"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[88] \"Do you know where Mrs. Jennings works for a lady, or where that big house is?\" Polly asked. [89] \"I don't know. I don't even know what Mrs. Jennings' last name is. I only know that she was deeply hurt years ago. That's what she said herself.\" [90] \"Hurt? Did she tell you what kind of harm it was?\" [91] \"Just one thing.\" Phryne said firmly. \"That's what broke her heart.\" [92] \"I mean, harm can be caused by many reasons, such as unrequited love or the untimely death of the one you love -\"\n[93] \"Oh, they don't know the details.\" Phryne continued, with a hint of resentment in her voice. \"She won't say, never. She only says that the hurt has been with her all along. That's just Janie, she never says much. Even if she were sitting here right now, you couldn't help her, and she's already gone. She lives in her own world, that's Janie's problem. She never talks about anything that happens.\" [94] The phrase \"living in her own world\" suddenly reminded Poirot of something - a conversation he had with Phryne a few weeks earlier on a Thursday evening at the \"Happy Coffee House.\" [95] So Poirot continued, \"She doesn't ask people questions either, right? Not interested in socializing or chatting, or caring about other people's gossip?\" [96] \"Absolutely right,\" Phoebe exclaimed. \"She has no curiosity. I've never seen anyone so self-absorbed, with no one else or the world in her eyes. She doesn't ask how others are doing or what they're up to.\" [97] \"I learned all that from your conversations with the other server, ma'am,\" Poirot said. [98] Phoebe's face flushed. \"I'm surprised you would listen to our conversations.\" [99] To avoid making Phoebe more uncomfortable, Poirot didn't tell her that he had actually been looking forward to hearing her talk about other clients and had even speculated about them. These people could be considered \"typical characters\" at the \"Joyful Coffee Shop,\" such as \"Mr. Unsure,\" who always came in, ordered food, but then canceled his order because he wasn't really hungry. [100] If the timing were right, Poirot would have liked to ask if Phoebe had given him a nickname like \"Mr. Unsure\" behind his back, perhaps something related to his neatly trimmed mustache. [101] \"So, Miss Jennings has no interest in others,\" Poirot mused. \"Unlike others who may not like to pry into the lives and thoughts of those around them but indulge in lengthy self-praise \u2013 she's not like that, is she?\" [102] Phyllida raised an eyebrow. \"You've got a good memory, and you're right. Miss Jennings doesn't like to talk about herself, but she'll answer questions without saying much. She doesn't like to dwell on anything. She has her own little secrets \u2013 ones that make her unhappy but keep her entangled. I gave up trying to figure her out a long time ago.\" [103] \"She's ensnared in hurt and danger,\" Poirot murmured. [104] \"Did she say she was in danger?\" [105] \"Yes, madam. Unfortunately, I was too slow to stop her from leaving. If something had happened to her\u2026\" Poirot shook his head, trying to dispel the growing sense of foreboding. \"I'll be back tomorrow morning, you said she often comes here, right? I'll find her before the danger arrives. This time, Hercule Poirot will be swift.\" [106] \"Speed doesn't matter,\" said Phryne. \"The problem is that no one can find Madame Jenny. Even if she's right in front of you, you won't find her. No one can help her. She stood up, picked up Poirot's plate, and concluded with, \"It's a shame to let such good food go cold.\" [107] * * *\n[108] Chapter 2\n[109] Three Rooms of Murder\n[110] The original text is in French. The French parts of the book are indicated in italics. [111] It began like this: It was Thursday, February 7, 1929, and the characters were Hercule Poirot, Jeanne, and Phryne Sprynge, in the crooked, teapot-filled \"Caf\u00e9 Joyeux.\" [112] Or I should say, it seemed like the beginning. I don't believe there's an actual beginning or end to real-life events. From any angle, you can trace the beginning of things back to a very long time ago, and then they continue on into the distant future. No one can say definitively, \"That's where it starts,\" and draw a line in the sand. [113] Fortunately, real stories always have a hero and a heroine. There won't be just me, and there won't be just one person, and I know for certain that those people are real. [114] That Thursday evening, I wasn't in the caf\u00e9. Although my name was mentioned - Edward Catchpole, Poirot's friend in Scotland Yard, thirty-two years old (to be precise, thirty-two) - I wasn't there. To fill in the gaps where I wasn't, I felt it necessary and appropriate to record Jeanne's story. Luckily, I have Poirot's testimony, the best witness. [115] I wrote these not for anyone else, but for myself. After each case, I read them again and again until I could read them with a calm mind, without the initial shock of \"How could this happen?\" to \"Yes, this is what happened.\" [116] In a way, I should have come up with a better title than \"Jean's Story.\" It doesn't sound like a title. [117] I met Hercule Poirot only six weeks before the Thursday night mentioned above. He was planning to rent Mrs. Blanche's apartment. The little house was spacious and spotlessly clean, with a square exterior and an interior that was as feminine as possible: full of lace, ruffles, and trimmings. Sometimes I even worried that when I left for work, I would suddenly find a lavender tassel hanging from something in the living room, wrapped around my arm or shoe. [118] Unlike me, Poirot did not plan to stay there permanently, only temporarily. That evening, he told me, \"I'm here to relax and rest, at least for a month.\" He spoke firmly, as if he were afraid I would interrupt him. \"My brain works too fast,\" he explained to me, \"Thoughts come rushing in... I believe I can relax here.\" [119] When I asked him where he was from, I thought he would say \"France.\" It was only later that I found out he was Belgian, not French. At the time, he walked over to the window, pulled back the lace curtains, and pointed to a large, beautiful house about 300 yards away as an answer to the question. \"You live there?\" I asked, thinking he was joking. [120] \"Yes, I hope not too far away,\" Poirot said. \"It's even better that I can see it every day: what a beautiful view!\" He proudly gazed at the grand mansion. For a while, I even wondered if he had forgotten that I was still standing next to him. Then he went on to say, \"Travel is also a pleasant thing! Exciting, but not restful. But if I don't go out, Poirot's brain won't get a vacation! Worry will follow. It's easy to be found at home, whether by friends or strangers, always with very important things - always very important! - and my gray brain cells have to work again, without having the chance to store energy. So, I tell people that Poirot is leaving London for a while, but in fact, he found a familiar place to rest and be undisturbed.\" [121] As he spoke, I nodded along, as if everything sounded perfectly reasonable. I wondered if people just got stranger as they got older. [122] On Thursday night, Mrs. Eversleigh never cooked, and she went to her late husband's sister's house. So Poirot had to go to \"The Happy Caf\u00e9.\" He told me that during his time away, he didn't want to risk going to his usual places and asked me, \"My friend, can you recommend a place like yours, with good food?\" I recommended \"The Happy Caf\u00e9\": a small, odd place, but one that you would want to return to after visiting once. [123] On that particular Thursday night - the night Poirot and Jenny met, he came back at ten past ten, much later than usual. I sat by the fireplace in the living room, but I didn't feel warm at all. I heard the front door open and close, and then Mrs. Eversleigh and Poirot whispered to each other for a while. She must have been waiting for him in the hallway. [124] I couldn't hear what she said, but I could guess: she was anxious, and the reason for her anxiety was me. She had noticed something was wrong with me when she came back from her sister's house at nine thirty. I looked a little scary, like I hadn't eaten or slept in a while. She told me so directly. But I didn't know how someone could suddenly look like they hadn't eaten, maybe I was a little thinner than when I had breakfast. [125] At the time, she examined me from head to toe, from left to right, and then brought out everything she could think of to make me feel better - food, drinks, and even chatted with me. I politely refused her kindness, and she immediately suggested some strange things: a pillow stuffed with herbs, and a weird-smelling but supposedly effective thing in a deep blue bottle that I had to put in the bathwater. [126] I thanked her and declined her offers. She looked around the living room, trying to find something to force on me that could solve my problem, but she couldn't find anything. [127] Now, she was whispering to Poirot, probably trying to convince him to force me to accept her stinky blue bottle or herb pillow. [128] On Thursday evenings, Poirot usually returned from \"The Joyful Cafe\" at nine. Tonight, I returned from the Blaxland Hotel at nine fifteen, determined not to think about what had happened there and hoping for the usual routine of watching Poirot sitting in his favorite chair, chatting about trivial matters. [129] But he wasn't there. It left me feeling empty, as if the sky had fallen. Poirot was a man of routine, and he rarely deviated from it. He had once told me, \"Carter, it's the monotony of this routine that keeps one calm.\" But tonight, he was an hour and fifteen minutes late. [130] At nine thirty, I heard the sound of the front door opening, thinking it was him, but it was Mrs. U.N. Owen. I almost complained out loud. If you're anxious, the last thing you need is a companion who is not cheerful and overly concerned. [131] I couldn't convince myself to go back to the Brawnsham Hotel tomorrow, but I knew I had to. And that was the last thing I wanted to think about right now. [132] Now, Poirot was back, and I knew he would be worried about me, as Mrs. Bunscombe had informed him. I believed that I would be more comfortable without them around. Since there was no opportunity for any light-hearted conversation today, I decided to say nothing. [133] Poirot entered the living room still wearing his coat and hat, closing the door. I expected him to have a long list of questions, but he seemed somewhat carelessly said, \"It's quite late. I was out looking for a woman, Jane, hoping she's still alive and hasn't been murdered. I couldn't find anything.\" [134] He was worried, yes, but not about me or whether I had eaten or wanted to eat. This made me feel relieved. \"Looking?\" I asked. [135] \"Yes, I was looking for a woman, Jane, hoping she's still alive and hasn't been murdered.\" [136] \"Murdered?\" I feel the ground give way beneath me again. Poirot is a famous detective, and he's told me about some of the cases he's worked on. That's why I didn't intend to tell him about what I'm doing, since he wanted to take a break from those cases, but now he's said a word that makes me uneasy. [137] \"What does she look like?\" I ask him. \"Tell me about her. Maybe I've seen her somewhere. Especially if she's been murdered. I saw three people killed tonight, two women and a man, maybe it's the one you're talking about. The man didn't look like Jane, but the two women\u2026\"\n[138] \"My friend,\" Poirot interrupts me, his voice calm, and the despairing words have no effect. He takes off his hat and begins to unbutton his coat. \"Mrs. Blanche says you have a problem. Ha! I didn't notice. Tell Poirot what's happened.\" [139] \"Three people were killed,\" I say. \"Completely different from the previous cases. Two women and a man died in different rooms.\" [140] I've been a policeman for five years and have been working at Scotland Yard for nearly two years, and I've seen many violent deaths, most of which seemed to be caused by loss of control. Someone killed in a fit of rage, someone killed because of alcohol. The case at the Bluxton Hotel tonight is different, with three crimes committed in the same hotel on the same day. I believe that at least several months would have been needed to prepare for this. Each crime scene is a chilling work of art that I can't decipher. What frightens me is that this time I can't crack this cold, carefully planned murder with the methods I usually use against hooligans and thugs. [141] \"Undoubtedly, I'm upset now, but it won't change my theory,\" I thought. When I think about the three bodies arranged the same way, I shiver. I tell myself to handle the case normally, regardless of what it looks like on the surface, and not to be afraid. [142] \"Is it the same three rooms in the same house?\" Polo asked. [143] \"No, it's the Brawnsham Hotel, just a few steps from Piccadilly Circus. Do you know where it is?\" [144] \"No.\" [145] \"I've never been there before, and it's not a place where someone like me can go. It's too luxurious.\" [146] Poirot sat there, his back straight, muttering to himself, \"Three murders, the same hotel, different rooms.\" [147] \"Yes, and all today, in the evening, and the crime scenes were cleared up quickly.\" [148] \"Tonight? But you're at home. Why aren't you at the hotel? Has the killer been caught?\" [149] \"Unfortunately, not. I... I stopped talking and cleared my throat, deciding that it was enough to tell him the situation, without mentioning how much the crime scene affected my emotions or how I didn't want to stay at Bluxton Hotel for even five more minutes. [150] The three bodies lay neatly on the floor: arms by their sides, palms down, legs together. [151] \"Lying on the ground, dead bodies,\" these words brought back memories of a dark room I was forced into when I was a child. From then on, I never wanted to remember that again and hoped never to remember it in my life. [152] Weak hands, palms down. \"Edward, hold his hand.\" [153] \"Don't worry, there are many policemen there.\" To distract myself from that unpleasant memory, I shouted, \"I'll be back first thing tomorrow morning.\" Seeing Poirot waiting for further explanation, I added, \"I need to clear my head. Honestly, I've never seen such a bizarre case.\" [154] \"How bizarre?\" [155] \"Each victim, both of them, had the same thing in their mouths.\" [156] \"No.\" Poirot shook his finger. \"My friend, this is impossible. The same thing cannot be in the mouths of three people at the same time.\" [157] \"Three identical ones.\" I explained, \"Three cufflinks, they look like pure gold, with the same letter combination: PIJ. Poirot? Are you okay? You look -\"\n[158] \"Goodness!\" He stood up and began to pace around the room. \"My friend, you don't understand what this means. Yes, you definitely don't understand, because you don't know about my encounter with Miss Jennings. I must tell you what happened tonight so you will understand.\" [159] When Poirot said to tell the story quickly, he was different from most people. Every detail was important to him, whether it was hundreds of people dying in a fire or a small pit on a child's chin. No one could rush him to get to the point, so I sat firmly in my chair and listened to him tell the story in his own way. By the time he finished, I felt like I was at the scene of the crime - in fact, I knew more about it than when I was actually there. [160] \"How incredible!\" I said. \"It's too coincidental. Tonight, three murders occurred at the Brawnsham Hotel.\" [161] Poirot sighed. \"I don't think it's a coincidence, my friend. Coincidences do happen, but these events are clearly connected.\" [162] \"You mean one murder and fear of being murdered,\" I said. [163] \"No. That is another connection, but I mean other connections.\" Poirot suddenly stopped pacing and turned to me. \"You said that the three victims had a gold cufflink with the letter combination PIJ in their mouths, right?\" [164] \"Yes.\" [165] \"Miss Valentine clearly told me, 'You swear, if anyone finds me dead, you tell your police friend not to look for the killer. Don't let them talk! Never solve the case.' What do you think 'don't let them talk' means?\" [166] \"Mrs. Vane clearly told me, 'You swear that if anyone finds me dead, you tell your policeman friend not to look for the killer. Don't let them talk! Never solve the case.' What do you think 'Don't let them talk' means?\" [167] Poirot seemed to be joking, but it wasn't. I said, \"Well, it's pretty clear, isn't it? She was afraid of being killed and didn't want the killer to be punished. She thought she deserved it.\" [168] \"You only understood the literal meaning.\" Poirot said, sounding disappointed in my answer, \"Ask yourself, 'Don't let them talk' has another meaning. Think about it from the perspective of your three gold cufflinks.\" [169] \"It's not mine.\" I emphasized, hoping to put the case aside, \"Okay, I know what you mean, but...\"\n[170] \"What do you know? What do I mean?\" [171] \"Indeed! 'Please don't let anyone open their mouths, don't find the gold cufflink with the PIJ combination.' Isn't that what Jean meant? She knew there would be three guests killed in the hotel, and she knew the killer would kill her too?\" [172] Before I could answer, Poirot continued his imaginative thinking.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nTo naturally meet you in the caf\u00e9, Mr. Poirot... Harriet can't pull that off. Richard and Ada can't either. So you see, I must live to the end. [1899] But the plans that Harriet and Ada knew were not Richard's true plans. Two weeks after meeting with Harriet and Ada, Richard came to London to see me alone, at which time he told me that the question of whether Nancy should die had been weighing heavily on him. He shared my belief that Nancy had not confessed to Harriet about the conversation and that there was no other reason for her to confess at the White Hart Inn other than to clear Patrick's name from the rumors. [1900] On the other hand, Richard also understood what Harriet meant. The death of Patrick and Francis Eve was caused by the misjudgment of multiple people, and Nancy was also not blameless. [1901] When Richard admitted that he couldn't make a decision about Nancy and decided to leave it up to me, I was shocked and terrified beyond measure. He said that after Harriet, Ada, and he were dead, I could choose whether to try my best to have Nancy hanged or commit suicide and leave a completely different note. Not one saying \"May they rest in peace,\" but one revealing the truth of the death. [1902] I begged Richard not to let me make the decision alone. I kept asking him, \"Why choose me?\". [1903] \"Because, Jenny,\" he said, and I'll never forget what he said next, \"because you are the best among us. And you never let it go to your head. Yes, you've lied, but as soon as you said it, you realized you were wrong. I've believed your lies for too long, and that's unforgivable. I've fueled the fire, supporting everyone to accuse an innocent good man. Yes, he had his faults, but who among us is perfect?\" [1904] \"Well,\" I told Richard, \"I'll make the decision and fulfill your wish.\" [1905] And so, our plan was set. Now, do you want to know how we managed to mess everything up? [1906] Chapter Twenty\n[1907] How We Screwed It All Up\n[1908] \"Indeed, we'd like to know,\" said Poirot. \"Both Casterpoole and I are curious.\" [1909] \"It's all my fault,\" said Jenny's voice, hoarse. \"I'm a coward, I really fear death. Without Patrick, my life was miserable, but I got used to the pain and didn't want to end it. Any kind of life, even filled with torment, is better than death! Please don't condemn me for violating Christian teachings, but I truly don't believe in an afterlife. As the day of execution drew nearer, I grew increasingly afraid, afraid to kill. I imagined what it would be like: standing in a locked room, watching Richard drink the poison, and I didn't want to do it. But I agreed! I promised.\" [1910] \"A few months ago, the plan seemed so easy to implement, now it seems so difficult,\" said Poirot. \"Of course, you couldn't tell Richard Nielsen how much you feared it, as he held you in high regard. If you admitted your fear, he might have looked down on you. Perhaps you were also worried that he might kill you regardless of your consent.\" [1911] \"Yes! I'm really afraid he'll do that. You see, from our conversations, I realized how important it is for him that the four of us die. He once told me that if Harry and Ada couldn't be convinced, he would 'do what he had to do without their consent.' That's what he said. He's so decisive, how can I tell him I've changed my mind and don't want to die or kill?\" [1912] \"I suppose you feel guilty about it too, ma'am. You also think that this murder and death are honorable and right, don't you?\" [1913] \"Rationally, yes, I do think so,\" said Jane. \"I hope and pray that I can find the courage to complete this task.\" [1914] \"How do you plan to deal with Nancy Duane?\" I asked Jane. [1915] \"I don't know. The panic from our first meeting that night has not subsided, Mr. Poirot. I haven't made any decisions! I let Semmi continue to fabricate the story about the key and to point the finger at Nancy. I let things take their natural course and tell myself that I can always go to the authorities and save her. But... I didn't do that. Richard thinks I'm better suited to do this, but he's wrong\u2014dead wrong.\" [1916] \"I still envy Nancy because Patrick loves her, and that was the root of the disaster at Grelling's. And... I know that if I admit to conspiring to frame an innocent woman, I'll go to jail. I'm scared.\" [1917] \"Lady, tell us, what did you do? On that day... the day of the 'execution', what did you do at the Browns Hotel? \" [1918] \"I was supposed to be there at six, the time we had agreed to meet.\" [1919] \"Do you mean the four conspirators?\" [1920] \"Yes, and Semmy. All day, I watched the clock tick, waiting for that terrible moment to come. At five o'clock, I realized I couldn't do it. I couldn't! So I didn't go to the hotel. I ran through the streets of London in fear, not knowing where to go or what to do, just running. I thought Richard Nichol would come looking for me, and he would be angry because I disappointed him and everyone else. I went to 'The Happy Cafe', thinking that since I couldn't kill Richard as promised, at least I could complete this part.\" [1921] \"When I got to the cafe, I started to worry about my life. Of course, nothing happened, you saw it yourself. I thought Richard, not Nancy, would kill me. What was even more terrifying was that I believed that if he killed me, he would be doing the right thing. I deserved to die! I haven't told you a single lie, M. Poirot, please recall what I said. I was afraid to be killed? Yes, I was afraid of Richard coming to kill me. Have I done terrible things in the past? Yes. And if Richard had really caught and killed me, I believed it was only a matter of time, and I didn't want him to be punished. I know I disappointed him. Can you understand that? Richard may have been seeking death, but I wanted him to live. Although he had hurt Patrick, he was a good man.\" [1922] \"Yes, Madam.\" [1923] \"M. Poirot, that night, I wanted to tell you the truth, but I didn't have the courage.\" [1924] \"So, you believe that Richard Nigges would have found you and killed you because you didn't go to Brocks Hotel to kill him that day?\" [1925] \"Yes. I think he wouldn't have been satisfied with dying without knowing why I didn't show up at the hotel.\" [1926] \"Indeed.\" I said, seething with anger. [1927] Jane nodded. [1928] I finally understood why the scene was as it was: the three bodies were in identical positions, between the small coffee table and the chairs, upright with feet facing the door. As Mr. Poirot said, it was impossible for Harriet Spear, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nixes to all end up in that position. [1929] The similarities between the three murder scenes were suspicious, and now I understood why: the conspirators wanted the police to believe there was only one killer. On the same night, at the same hotel, three bodies, with a cufflink in each mouth. Any competent detective could have deduced this was the work of one person, and an obsessive one at that. But they knew there were more than one killer, so they were afraid, afraid of the truth being revealed, so they went to great lengths to make the three murder scenes look very similar, even overdoing it. [1930] The bodies lying neatly on the ground, upright and identical, also aligned with their intentions: what happened at Browns Hotel wasn't a murder, but an execution. After each execution, there were subsequent procedures, like a religious ritual. I thought it was better to arrange the bodies properly than to leave them lying around, not looking like the work of an ordinary killer or a garden killer. [1931] Suddenly, a younger Jane Hobbs appeared in my mind, tidying up the beds in the rooms at Jesus College, Cambridge, one by one. She would arrange each bed in the same pattern...\n[1932] Beds, death beds...\n[1933] Pattern, disrupted pattern...\n[1934] \"Richard Naylegh committed suicide.\" I blurted out involuntarily, \"It was suicide. He wanted it to look like murder, so he followed the pattern of the other two, he wanted us to think it was the same killer, but he had to lock the door from the inside. That's why he hid the key behind the tiles in the fireplace, pretending that the killer took the key and then escaped through the window. If the key was found, we would have been suspicious - and we were indeed puzzled - why the killer locked the door from the inside and hid the key in the room, yet escaped through the window. But we would still believe there was only one killer. Naylegh only cared about that. But if the window was closed and the key was found, we would only conclude that Richard Naylegh committed suicide. He would not take the risk of letting us draw that conclusion, right? Once we came to that conclusion, the plot to frame Nancy Duane for the murders of three people would fail, and we would be more likely to believe that Naylegh first killed Harriet Spear and Ada Gransbury and then committed suicide.\" [1935] \"Yes,\" Jane said, \"I think you're right.\" [1936] \"The position of the cufflinks is different...\" murmured Poirot, raising his eyebrows at me to continue. [1937] \"The cufflinks were so close to Nigges' throat because he convulsed after taking the poison, causing his mouth to open. He had to be careful to swallow the poison while keeping his body straight, placing the cufflinks between his lips, but they slipped into his mouth. Unlike Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransberry, Richard Nigges died without anyone helping him arrange the scene, so the cufflinks couldn't be placed in the same position as planned.\" [1938] \"Do you think Mr. Nigges would have taken the poison, not knowing why you weren't at the hotel?\" Poirot asked Jenny. [1939] \"I never believed it until I read the news of his death in the paper.\" [1940] \"Ah.\" Poirot's expression was inscrutable. [1941] \"For a long time, Richard had been looking forward to ending his life on Thursday night, putting an end to years of guilt and torment.\" Mrs. Vane said, \"I believe he went to the Blaxland Hotel to die, so even if I hadn't shown up to kill him, he had completed his self-inflicted death.\" [1942] \"Thank you, madam.\" Poirot stood up, almost losing his balance due to sitting for too long. [1943] \"What will you do with me, Mr. Poirot?\" [1944] \"Please stay at home until I or Mr. Cattermole visit again. If you run away again, it will be even worse for you.\" [1945] \"Even if I stay here, things won't get better.\" Mrs. Vane said, her eyes filled with despair and emptiness. \"Yes, Mr. Cattermole, you shouldn't worry about me. I'm prepared for what's to come.\" [1946] Without a doubt, she said this to reassure me, but it made me feel fear. Her attitude seemed to see the future, to see how terrible things would happen. But whatever happens, I know I'm not ready and don't want to be ready. [1947] Chapter 21\n[1948] All demons\n[1949] All day long, Poirot didn't speak, except for repeating twice that we must go to Grelling immediately and not waste any time. He looked preoccupied and obviously didn't want to talk. [1950] We returned to our residence and saw Stanley Bill waiting for us. Poirot asked, \"What's the matter? You've come because of my artwork, haven't you?\" [1951] \"What, sir? Oh, your artwork? No, that's fine, sir. Actually\u2026\" Bill took an envelope out of his pocket and handed it to Poirot, \"This has the answer you wanted.\" [1952] \"Thank you, officer. But there must be something, right? You look anxious. Is it about that?\" [1953] \"Yes, sir. I came to deliver a message. A man named Ambrose Flavard went to Scotland Yard to see Mr. Cargraves. He said he's the doctor from Gregholing village and needs to see him urgently, saying he can help.\" [1954] Poirot looked at me and then back at Stanley Bill, saying, \"We were just about to go there. Do you know why he wants to see Cargraves?\" [1955] \"I'm afraid I do, sir. It's not good news, sir. A woman named Margaret Ernst was attacked and is dying\u2026\"\n[1956] \"Oh, no.\" I muttered. [1957] \"And she wants to see Mr. Cader before she dies. Sir, considering what Dr. Flavard said, I suggest you hurry. The car is outside and can take you to the station.\" [1958] Considering Poirot's methodical and dislike of chaos, I said, \"Can we give us half an hour to prepare?\" [1959] Bill looked at his watch and said again, \"Sir, we have at most five to ten minutes, we can't waste time, otherwise, you'll miss the next train.\" [1960] I had to admit, this time Poirot was faster than me, he had already packed his luggage and was hurrying me downstairs, saying, \"Hurry up, my friend.\" [1961] In the car, I decided not to remain silent, even though I knew Poirot didn't want to talk. \"If I didn't go to that damn village, Margaret Ernst wouldn't have been attacked.\" I said coldly, \"Someone must have seen me go to her house and stay there for a long time.\" [1962] \"Long enough for her to tell you everything, or almost everything. But since she has already told the police everything, what's the point of killing her?\" [1963] \"Revenge, punishment. Honestly, it doesn't make sense. If Nancy Duane is innocent, then Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid are the masterminds behind it - I mean the living masterminds, then why would Jenny and Kid kill Margaret Ernst? Margaret's words won't threaten them, and she never harmed Patrick and Francis Ives.\" [1964] \"I agree with your point of view. I also don't think Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid want to kill Margaret Ernst.\" [1965] The rain pattered on the car window, making it difficult for us to hear each other and concentrate. I asked again, \"Who could it be? I thought all the problems were solved -\"\n[1966] \"Carter, you can't really believe that, can you?\" [1967] \"Yes, I do. I guess you'll say I'm wrong, but didn't all the problems get solved? Before we knew Margaret Ernst was attacked, everything was clear.\" [1968] \"You actually think everything is clear!\" Poirot looked out the window at the rain pounding against the glass, chuckling foolishly. [1969] \"Well, to me, it's quite clear. All the killers are dead. Edith killed Harry with her consent, and Richard Nix killed her willingly. Since Jeanne didn't kill Nix as planned, he chose to commit suicide. Jeanne Hobbs and Samuel Kid didn't kill anyone. Of course, they were involved in the murder, but I think it's not really a murder, but \u2013\"\n[1970] \"Voluntarily sentenced to death?\" [1971] \"Exactly.\" [1972] \"What an ingenious plan, isn't it? Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, Richard Nigges, and Jenny Hobbs, let's call them A, B, C, and D, and see how clever their plan was.\" [1973] \"Why not use their names?\" I asked. [1974] Poirot didn't answer me. \"A, B, C, and D, all four had guilt and sought redemption for their souls. They all agreed that they would use their lives to make up for the sins they had committed, so they planned to kill each other: B killed A, then C killed B, and finally D killed C.\"\n[1975] \"But D didn't kill C, did she? D represents Jenny Hobbs, who didn't kill Richard Nigges.\" [1976] \"Perhaps, but the plan was for her to do so. Moreover, D must live long enough to see E \u2013 Nancy Drew, who was hanged for murdering A, B, and C. By then, D\u2026\" He suddenly stopped, said \"D\" again, and then said, \"Demise, that's the correct answer.\" [1977] \"What?\" [1978] \"Your crossword puzzle. A six-letter word meaning 'death', do you remember? I said the answer was 'murder', you said no, unless 'murder' began with\u2026\" He stopped again, shaking his head. [1979] \"If 'murder' had started with D, that would have been perfect. Yes, I remember. Poirot, are you all right? His eyes glinted with strange green light, which occasionally occurred. [1980] \"What? Of course! If 'murder' had started with D, that would have been perfect! Yes! That's it! My friend, you've done me a great favor! Now I believe\u2026yes, that's the truth. The truth must be like this. The old couple \u2013 ah, everything is clear now!\" [1981] \"Please explain what's going on.\" [1982] \"Okay, okay, but I need to prepare myself first.\" [1983] \"What's not ready? What are you waiting for?\" [1984] \"Kattakur, you at least need to give me twenty seconds to collect my thoughts and organize my language. I need time to explain to someone who's completely in the dark, and I see you haven't understood anything yet. You say everything is clear, but the story Jenny Hobbs told us this morning was a carefully crafted lie! Don't you see that?\" [1985] \"Eh... Do you mean... \"\n[1986] \"Harriet Spear wants to pin three murders on Nancy Drew, making her hang, but would Richard Nixes really agree to that? And would he leave Nancy's fate in the hands of Jenny Hobbs? This leader, Richard Nixes, a beloved authority figure who has been guilty for sixteen years for unjustly attacking Patrick Eves, would he do that?\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHowever, while working with Nancy, Jenny pretended to be involved in another plot, making Richard Nix believe she was his accomplice.\" [2382] \"That doesn't sound 'simple' to me.\" I said, \"It's quite complex.\" [2383] \"No, no, friend, it's not complicated at all. Your problem is that you've mixed different versions of this story together. You have to forget everything Jenny said at Samuel Kid's house and erase it from your mind completely. She was lying from beginning to end, of course, I don't deny there was some truth in it. The most convincing lies have a grain of truth. Soon, Jenny will tell us the whole truth, she's not afraid of anything now. But first, my friend, I must commend you, it was your clue at the Saint's Churchyard that made me understand everything.\" [2384] Poirot turned to Jenny and said, \"You told Harriet Westphal that Patrick Yves was helping parishioners communicate with their deceased loved ones for money, and that Nancy Duane often went to the vicar's house at night to talk to her deceased husband William. How many times have I heard this terrible, wicked rumor? Many, many times. Miss Hobbs, you admitted to us that day that you said these lies out of jealousy and weakness. But that's not true!\" [2385] \"Standing in front of the desecrated grave of Patrick and Francis Yves, Kitcher told me, 'If Jenny Hobbs made up lies not to hurt Patrick Yves, but to help him?' Kitcher realized a key point that I took for granted. This has never been suspected, so I never verified it: that Harriet Westphal was crazy about her late husband George. Didn't Poirot know how much Harriet loved George? Didn't he also know how George's death turned Harriet from a happy, kind woman into a bitter, evil demon? It's hard for an outsider to imagine such a terrible, destructive disaster that could wipe out all joy and destroy all virtues. Yes, of course, I know Harriet Westphal has experienced such a disaster. I believe it, which made me not doubt! [2386] \"I also know that Jenny Hobbs loves Patrick Yves and is willing to sacrifice her own happiness and abandon her fianc\u00e9 Samuel Kid to continue serving the Yves couple. It's a selfless love, willing to pay without expecting anything in return. But in the story Jenny and Nancy told us, they said that Jenny lied out of jealousy, jealous of the love Patrick gave to Nancy. That's not possible! It's inconsistent! We must consider both feasibility and psychological factors. Patrick Yves married Francis without punishment from Jenny, accepting the reality that he belonged to another woman. She continued to be his faithful servant in the vicarage, helping him and his wife, and the couple treated her well. For years, Jenny had selflessly loved and served Patrick, so why did his love for Nancy suddenly drive her to lie and cause a series of events that ultimately destroyed him? The answer is, such a thing is impossible.\" [2387] \"It's not jealousy or longing that drove Jenny Hobbs to lie. It's a completely different reason. Miss Hobbs, you've been helping the man you love, haven't you? You could say, to save him. When my clever friend Kitteral said his theory, I realized what the truth was. It's so obvious, Poirot was so dull that he didn't see it!\" [2388] Jenny looked at me and asked, \"What theory?\" [2389] I opened my mouth to explain, but Poirot interrupted, \"When Harriet Cypers told you she saw Nancy Duane visiting the vicarage at night, you immediately sensed danger. You knew about their rendezvous \u2013 you lived in the vicarage, how could you not know? You were eager to protect Patrick Yves' good name. How could you protect his reputation? How could you explain Nancy Duane's late-night visits when Francis Yves was not at home? How could you fool the gossips? Just when you were about to give up hope, it seemed like magic, you suddenly thought of a way. You decided to use temptation and fantasy to eliminate Harriet's threat.\" [2390] Jenny said nothing, staring blankly into the distance. [2391] \"Miss Hariot Westcliff and Nancy Duane have one thing in common,\" Poirot continued. \"Their husbands died young. You told Miss Hariot Westcliff that Patrick Eves could help Nancy communicate with her late husband, William Duane, of course for a fee. Naturally, such a thing had to be kept secret and not revealed to the church or anyone in the village. But you hinted to Miss Hariot Westcliff that if she wanted, Patrick could also help her, just like Nancy, communicate with her husband's spirit. And she and George might\u2026 well, even if they couldn't be together again, at least they could communicate in some way. Tell me, what was her reaction when you told her this?\" [2392] There was a long silence before Jenny said, \"She was salivating, eager to communicate with her late husband. She said she would pay any amount of money to talk to George again. Mr. Poirot, you can't imagine how much she loved that man. I watched her face\u2026 it was like a dead woman coming back to life. I tried to explain the whole thing to Patrick, to tell him what had happened, but I had solved it. You see, I made a promise to Miss Hariot Westcliff without his consent. I knew Patrick wouldn't agree to this, but I had no other choice! I didn't want him to stop me. Can you understand that?\" [2393] \"I understand, madam.\" [2394] \"I hope I can convince him. He is a man of principles, but I know he wants to protect Frances and doesn't want this scandal to hurt her, and he also wants to protect Nancy. And this is the only way to keep Miss Hariot Westcliff silent, the only way! Patrick only had to occasionally comfort Miss Hariot Westcliff with some words, pretending they were from George Westcliff. And he didn't need to ask her for money. I told him all this, but he wouldn't listen. He found it terrifying.\" [2395] \"He would naturally feel that way.\" Poirot said softly. \"Please continue.\" [2396] \"He said that doing as I suggested would be unfair and immoral to Hattie, and would make him more likely to be destroyed. I begged him to reconsider. What harm could there be in making Hattie happy? But Patrick was firm, insisting that I tell Hattie that he couldn't do it. His instructions were clear: 'Jane, don't tell her you lied, or she'll suspect the whole thing is untrue.' He wanted me to tell Hattie that her wishes couldn't be fulfilled.\" [2397] \"And you had to tell her that, with no other choice,\" I said. [2398] \"There was truly no other choice. I told Hattie that Patrick had refused her request, and she immediately turned against him and spread my lies throughout the village. Actually, Patrick could have ruined her reputation, saying she had always wanted him to provide unethical religious services and that if he refused, she would defame him for desecrating God and violating doctrine. But he didn't do that. He said that no matter how maliciously Hattie attacked him, he wouldn't defame her reputation. How foolish! He could have made her shut up immediately, but his morals were too high, and he didn't do that!\" [2399] \"Was it at this point that you went to seek Nancy Drew's advice?\" [2400] \"Yes. I felt it shouldn't only be me and Patrick who were troubled. Nancy was also involved in this. I asked her if I should publicly admit that I had lied, but she advised me not to. She said, 'Jane, I'm worried that doing so will bring trouble to Patrick and me. Jane, the wisest thing to do is to retreat into the background, say nothing, and not sacrifice yourself. I'm not sure if you're strong enough to withstand Hattie's defamation.' She underestimated me. I was very unsettled at the time, and perhaps I looked on the verge of collapse, as I was too worried about Patrick and worried that Hattie would ruin him. But Mr. Poirot, I am not a weak person.\" [2401] \"I know you're not afraid.\" [2402] \"I'm not afraid. The news of Harry Sipper's death gave me a new sense of purpose. The killer did the world a favor by getting rid of that despicable hypocrite.\" [2403] \"Your words bring us back to the question: who killed Harry Sipper? You told us it was Edith Gransby, but that was a lie.\" [2404] \"I don't need to tell you the truth, Poirot sir, since you know it as well as I do.\" [2405] \"Then please have some pity on Mr. Cattermole, who still doesn't understand the whole situation.\" [2406] \"It would be best if you tell him, yes?\" Jane smiled, a weary smile. Suddenly, she seemed more ethereal to me, as if she might vanish at any moment. [2407] \"Very well,\" said Poirot. \"I'll start with Harriet Sipper and Edith Gransberry, two stubborn women who thought themselves just, pushing a good man to an early grave. Did they feel remorse for the deceased? No, on the contrary, they opposed burying him in a consecrated grave. After Richard Nigel's patient persuasion, did these two women regret treating Patrick Ives so badly? Of course not, they wouldn't. It was then, Jane, that I knew you were lying. The truth lies in your story.\" [2408] Jane shrugged and said, \"Anything is possible.\" [2409] \"No, only the truth is possible. I know that Harriet Sipper and Edith Gransberry would never willingly take their own lives. Therefore, they were killed by someone else. To say they committed suicide is easy! You think that if Poirot knew all the dead were willingly taking their own lives, his gray brain cells wouldn't work. It was their chance to repent! What an extraordinary, imaginative story, one that people would believe without question, who would suspect it was fabricated?\" [2410] \"It's my safety measure, to be used only when necessary,\" said Jane. \"I hope you never find me, but I'm afraid you will.\" [2411] \"If I find you, you will need an alibi from seven fifteen to eight ten to protect yourself, and Nancy Drew as well. You and Samuel Kid would only be accused of framing an innocent woman, but not of murder or conspiracy to murder. That's a clever move, admitting to doing wrong to avoid a more serious charge. You eliminated your enemies, and no one would suffer for it, as long as we believed your story \u2013 that Edith Garnsey killed Harriet Spear, Richard Nigger killed Edith Garnsey, and then committed suicide. Lady, your plan was brilliant, but it still outsmarts Hercule Poirot!\" [2412] \"Richard wanted to die.\" Jane said angrily, \"He was not killed, he was seeking death.\" [2413] \"Yes.\" Poirot said, \"That is the only truth in this whole lie.\" [2414] \"It was all his fault. I would not have killed anyone if it weren't for Richard.\" [2415] \"But you did kill, and not just once. It was Cacophony's careless remark that led me to the clue.\" [2416] \"What did he say?\" asked Jane. [2417] \"He said, if the murder started with D\u2026\"\n[2418] Hearing Poirot thank me made me feel a little uneasy. I didn't understand why those few casual words were so important. [2419] Poirot began to speak again: \"Lady, after hearing your story and leaving Samuel Kidd's house, we naturally discussed what you said: the plan you made with Richard Nigges\u2026 Allow me to say that this plan is quite attractive. Simple and clean, like a line of dominoes. But after thinking about it again, I realized that the order of events had changed. It wasn't D down, then C, then B, and finally A; it was B who knocked A down, and then C knocked B down\u2026 But that's not the point.\" [2420] What was he talking about? Jane seemed to have understood it even less. [2421] \"Ah, I must explain it more clearly,\" Poirot said. \"Lady, to make it easier to imagine the order of the murder, I used letters to replace the names. In the story you told us at Samuel Kid's house, the plan was that B killed A, then C killed B, then D killed C, and then D waited for E to be condemned and hanged for murdering A, B, and C, and finally D committed suicide. That is, in your story, you are the D. Understand, Miss Hobbs?\" [2422] Jenny nodded. [2423] \"Good. Mr. Cattermole is a crossword puzzle enthusiast, and at that moment, his interests coincided with mine, and he asked me to think of a word with six letters that meant 'death'. I said 'murder'. Mr. Cattermole said it was not. He said, 'If \"murder\" starts with D, it fits in.' Later, I remembered his words and dared to guess what would happen if the murder really started with D? If the first murderer was not Adela Gransbury, but you, Miss Hobbs?\" [2424] \"As time went on, I came to the conclusion that this was the case. I also knew why you definitely killed Harriet Sipper. From Grilling to the Bluxton Hotel, Harriet and Adela neither took the same train nor the same car, so they didn't know each other's existence, which means there was no such a plan of one killing the other. It was all lies.\" [2425] \"What is the truth?\" I asked eagerly. [2426] \"Harriet Sipper and Edith Garnet both thought they were going to London alone for personal reasons. Harriet was called by Jane, who said she urgently needed to see her and asked for absolute secrecy. Jane told Harriet that she had booked a room for her at the Bluxton Hotel and that she would meet her between 3:30 and 4:00 pm on Thursday to discuss important matters. Harriet accepted Jane's invitation because the reasons mentioned in the invitation letter were irresistible. [2427] \"You offered her something that Patrick Ivor had refused to give her years ago, didn't you, Miss? Communication with her late husband. You told her that her husband George Sipper wanted to communicate with her through you, that he had tried sixteen years ago but failed. Now, George wants to send a message to his beloved wife through you, the medium. He has spoken to you from the other side! Oh, I believe you made a convincing case! Harriet could not resist the temptation and would believe you because she desperately wanted it to be true. She believed the lie you told her years ago that spirits could communicate with the living people they loved, and she never doubted it. [2428] \"You are a clever old man, M. Poirot,\" said Jane. \"Full marks!\" [2429] \"Kitchener, tell me, do you now understand who the old woman and the young man who could be her son in room 317 were talking about? You were always confused about who Southy and Samuel Kid were talking about in room 317.\" [2430] \"I can't say I understand. Oh, I still don't understand.\" [2431] \"Let's review what Rafael Bockarov said. He heard South Nancy Dunn impersonating Harriet Westbury say, 'He no longer trusts her. He doesn't like her now. It was her choice to go. She's old enough to be his mother now.' Think about the phrase 'He doesn't like her now.' That's the premise, and then comes the two reasons for the 'not liking.' One of them is that she's old enough to be his mother now. No, 'Now, she's old enough to be his mother.' Don't you understand, Carter? If she's old enough to be his mother now, she should have always been old enough to be his mother. There's no other possibility!\" [2432] \"Isn't it just a long sentence?\" I said. \"I mean, the sentence can still make sense without the word 'now.' 'He doesn't like her. It was her choice to go. She's old enough to be his mother.'\" [2433] \"But, my friend, that would be absurd!\" Poirot exclaimed. \"It's illogical. The sentence has the word 'now,' and we can't pretend it's not there, ignore the word 'now,' it's right there in our ears!\" [2434] \"I'm afraid I can't agree with your point of view,\" I said uneasily. \"If I must try to think about it, I would say that the sentence is trying to convey that before she left, the young man didn't particularly care or notice the age difference between them, perhaps not so obvious. Now that she's no longer youthful, he has moved on and fallen in love with a younger, more attractive woman and trusts her\u2014\"\n[2435] Poirot couldn't help but interrupt my words, his face turning red with impatience. \"In my opinion, your guess is absurd, Carter! Listen to me! Listen to the original sentence again, the order of the words is like this, 'He doesn't like her now, it was her choice to go, she's old enough to be his mother now!' First, he doesn't like her, then comes the second reason! The sentence structure is clear, both of these unfortunate reasons are 'now' situations, not the past.\" [2436] \"You don't have to yell at me, Poirot. I understand your point, but I still can't agree. Not everyone is as concerned with word choice as you are. My interpretation must be correct, yours is wrong, just as you said, there's no other possibility. You just said it yourself, if she's old enough to be his mother now, then she should have always been old enough to be his mother.\" [2437] \"Ketsepalu, Ketsepalu, I'm starting to lose hope in you! Think about what comes after this sentence. Raffles Burbank heard Samuel Kid, pretending to be Richard Nix, say, 'I don't agree with what you just said about being old enough to be his mother, I don't agree at all.' In response, Nancy Drew, pretending to be Harriet, said, 'Well, neither of us can prove we're right, so let's agree to disagree.' Why can't they prove themselves right? Is a woman old enough to be a man's mother not a simple biological fact? If she's only four years older than him, of course, she's not old. Is that not obvious? If she's twenty years older, then she's old enough to be his mother. That's also indisputable.\" [2438] \"What if she's thirteen years older?\" Jean Hobbs closed her eyes and said, \"Or twelve? There are always exceptions... Of course, I'm not talking about this particular case.\" [2439] It seems that Jean understands Poirot's point, but I don't. [2440] \"Thirteen years, twelve years, it doesn't matter!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf you ask a doctor or a medical expert, theoretically, can a twelve or thirteen-year-old girl have a child? The answer is either 'yes' or 'no.' Let's not get caught up in the age at which a woman can have a child! Remember Samuel Kid's comment about the young man, it was quite amusing. He said, 'Does he have a brain? I don't think he does.' You would think that Kid's meaning was that the young man was a fool.\" [2441] \"Yes, that's exactly what I think,\" I said impatiently. \"Since you're so much smarter than me, why don't you just tell me where I'm wrong?\" [2442] Polo grumbled discontentedly. \"My goodness! The couple in Room 317 were Harriet Sipper and her husband George. It wasn't a serious debate, it was just joking. George Sipper died when they were both young, and Samuel Kid said he didn't have a brain, meaning that if he existed after death, he wouldn't be in human form, but in a soul, right? Souls don't have human brains, so George Sipper's soul couldn't have a brain either.\" [2443] \"I\u2026 oh, my goodness. Yes, now I understand.\" [2444] \"Samuel Kid began his argument with 'I think\u2026' because he knew that Nancy Duane would disagree with him. She might say, 'Souls certainly have ideas, and souls can be attached to people, don't they have free will? Where does their will come from if they don't have a brain?'\" [2445] From a philosophical perspective, this is an interesting point of view. In other circumstances, I might also express my own opinion.\" [2446] Poirot continued, \"Nancy said 'old enough to be his mother' because she believed that when a person dies, their age is fixed forever. In the afterlife, they won't age. If George Sipper's soul could come back to visit his wife, he would still be a twenty-something-year-old, the same age he was when he died. And she, as a forty-something-year-old woman, is now old enough to be his mother.\" [2447] \"Great!\" Jenny exclaimed sincerely, \"I wasn't there at the time, but they argued again in front of me later. Mr. Poirot, you are truly insightful. Mr. Kitchener, I hope you appreciate him.\" She then turned to Poirot and said, \"Their argument never ends...oh, forever! Nancy insists she's right, and Sem doesn't back down. He says souls are not limited by time dimensions, they don't have a concept of time, so you can't say someone is old enough to be a soul's mother.\" [2448] Poirot said to me, \"How disgusting, isn't it, Mr. Kitchener? When Rafael Buccar went to deliver the dessert, Nancy Drew sat next to the corpse of Ida Gransberry propped up on a chair, mocking another woman who died that day due to their conspiracy. Poor and foolish Harryette, her husband had no interest in communicating with her directly from the grave, he only wanted to talk to Jenny Hobbs. And Harryette had no choice, if she wanted to receive his message, she had to go to Bluxton to see Jenny. But going to see Jenny meant certain death.\" [2449] \"No one deserves to be killed more than Harryette Sipper.\" Jenny said, \"I regret many things, but I don't regret killing Harryette.\" [2450] \"What about Ida Gransberry?\" I asked, \"Why did she go to the Bluxton Hotel?\" [2451] \"Ah!\" Poirot said, always eager to share his seemingly endless knowledge with others, \"Ada also received an irresistible invitation from Richard Nigges. Not to speak with the deceased lover, but to meet her separated for sixteen years' ex-fianc\u00e9. It's not hard to imagine how tempting that is. Richard Nigges abandoned Ada, which undoubtedly broke her heart. She remained single for life. I think he hinted at reconciliation, even possibly marriage, in his letter. Ada agreed \u2013 who wouldn't give love another chance? Richard told her he would come to her room at Bluxton Hotel on Thursday from 3:30 to 4:00 pm. Ketteral, do you remember saying that you would go to the hotel on Wednesday so that you could spend Thursday preparing for the murder? Does it make more sense now?\" [2452] I nodded and said, \"Nigges knew he was going to commit murder on Thursday and that he would be killed himself. To prepare mentally for the double test, he naturally wanted to be on the scene a day earlier.\" [2453] \"Also to avoid any delays that could hinder the plan,\" Poirot said. [2454] \"So, Jane Hobbs killed Harriet Seppel, and Richard Nigges killed Ada Gransberry?\" I asked. [2455] \"Yes, my friend,\" Poirot said, looking at Jane, who nodded. \"I believe the murders in rooms 121 and 317 were carried out around the same time. I suspect the methods were the same, luring Harriet and Ada to drink poison. Jane told Harriet, 'I think you need to drink some water before I tell you what I have to say. Sit down, and I'll get you some water.' They took cups from the sink to fill with water, and they slipped in the poison. Then they handed the poisoned water to the victims, who soon died.\" [2456] \"How did Richard Naygreaves die?\" I asked. [2457] \"According to their previous plan, Jeanne was supposed to kill him,\" said Poirot. [2458] \"Most of what I said to you at Semple's was true,\" said Jeanne. \"Richard did write to me after many years. He was tormented by what he had done to Patrick and Francis. He couldn't find a way out, couldn't make amends, couldn't find inner peace, unless we all paid with our lives. We all had a responsibility.\" [2459] \"He made you... help him kill Harriet and Ida?\" I asked, trying to clarify the situation. [2460] \"Yes, they, and myself,\" said Jeanne. \"He insisted that we all had to die, otherwise it wouldn't have any meaning. He didn't want to be a murderer, he wanted to be an executor - he used that word several times, which means that neither of us could escape punishment. I agreed that Harriet and Ida should die, they were too evil. But... I didn't want to die, nor did I want Richard to die. I think what he did was sincere repentance for what he had done, and that was enough for Patrick, and for any higher power that might exist or not exist. But I couldn't convince Richard. I soon realized that there was no point in trying to convince him. He was as clever as ever, but he had lost some parts that made him paranoid, strange, and come up with many absurd ideas. He never forgot that event, those sins... He became a strange madman. I knew that if I didn't comply with his proposal, he would kill me. He didn't say it outright because he didn't want me to be coerced. You know, he was very good to me, he just needed an ally, someone who shared his views. He sincerely believed that I would agree to his plot because he thought I was more reasonable than Harriet and Ida. He was too confident in his own opinion, believing that only by sacrificing our four lives could he atone for his sins. I think he may have been right, but I was afraid. But now I'm not afraid anymore, and I don't know what changed me. Maybe, despite living in pain all these years, I still held a glimmer of hope that things would get better. Sadness does not equal despair.\" [2461] \"You knew you had to pretend to comply to save your life,\" said Poirot. \"Perfectly deceiving Richard Nix was the only way to escape death. But you didn't know how, so you turned to Nancy Drew.\" [2462] \"Yes, I went to her. She helped me, or so I thought. Her plan was brilliant. She suggested that I change one part of the plan. Richard's original plan was that once Harriet and Ada were dead, he would kill me and then commit suicide. Naturally, as someone who enjoyed being in control and being the authority, he wanted to be in charge from start to finish. [2463] \"Nancy told me to convince Richard that I should kill him, not the other way around. 'Impossible!' I said, 'He won't agree.' But Nancy said he would, as long as I presented it the right way. She advised me to act more obsessed with our goal than he was. She was right; it worked. I went to Richard and told him that it wasn't enough for just the four of us\u2014me, him, Harriet, and Ada\u2014to die. Nancy had to be punished too. I told him that Nancy was even more evil than Harriet, and how she had plotted to steal Patrick away from his wife and wouldn't stop until she succeeded. I also told him that Nancy had confessed to me that her speech at the King's Head Inn wasn't meant to help Patrick, but to hurt Francis. Nancy wanted Francis to commit suicide or at least give up Patrick and return to Cambridge to be with her father, so she could take his place.\" [2464] \"More lies,\" said Poirot. [2465] \"Yes, more lies\u2014but these were Nancy's ideas, and they worked! Richard agreed that I should die first.\" [2466] \"And he didn't know about Samuel Kid, did he?\" said Poirot. [2467] \"No, he didn't. Nancy and I brought in Sem, who was part of our plan. Neither Nancy nor I wanted to climb out the window, down the tree, and risk breaking our necks after locking the door and hiding the key behind the tiles. But that was the only way out of Room 238, so we needed Samuel's help. He not only did that but also impersonated Richard.\" [2468] \"Hiding the key behind the tiles,\" I murmured to myself, confirming the details in my mind. \"So, the story you told us at Mr. Kid's house was seamless, Richard Naylors hiding the key to make us think the killer took it, so he could frame Nancy Drew for the murder at the Blaux Han hotel.\" [2469] \"That's right,\" said Poirot. \"More precisely, he thought that was the case. When Jenny gave him the poisoned water, he still believed Jenny would live and ensure that Nancy Drew was accused of the murder at the Blaux Han Hotel. He believed Jenny would report to the police and make sure they suspected Nancy. But he didn't know that Nancy had prepared a solid alibi, that she was with the Wallace couple at that time! He also didn't know that a brooch would be placed in his mouth, that the key would be hidden behind the tiles, and that the windows would be open...\"\n[2470] \"Yes, Richard didn't know all of that,\" agreed Jenny. \"You now understand why I said Nancy's plan was brilliant, Mr. Poirot.\" [2471] \"She's a talented artist, madam. A clever artist who can see how to design details and also grasp the framework, knowing how to integrate all the elements together.\" [2472] Jenny turned to me and said, \"Nancy and I didn't want to do this, you have to believe me, Mr. Ketchup. If I had opposed Mr. Richard's plan, he would have killed me.\" She sighed. \"We planned it all, Nancy would escape unharmed, and Nancy and I would be punished for framing and accusing her, but not with the death penalty. We hoped to serve a few months in prison. After that, I planned to marry Sammy.\" [2473] \"Madam, everything was already ruined. I know you killed Harriet Sipper and Richard Nigges.\" [2474] \"I didn't kill Richard, Mr. Poirot. You're wrong about that.\" [2475] \"Yes, but you deceived him. Richard Nigges agreed to die because you agreed to participate in his plan, that all four of you would die. Then you brought Nancy Dunn into it, and the plan became five people. But you didn't really agree to his plan. You betrayed him and set up another trap behind his back. Who knows if Richard Nigges would have chosen to die in that way if he had known about your secret agreement with Nancy Dunn?\" [2476] Jane's expression stiffened. \"I didn't kill Richard Nigg. I killed him in self-defense, or he would have killed me.\" [2477] \"You said he didn't explicitly say he would kill you.\" [2478] \"No, but I knew he would. What do you think, Mr. Cattermole? Did I kill Richard Nigg?\" [2479] \"I don't know.\" [2480] \"Cattermole, my friend, don't be silly.\" [2481] \"He's not silly.\" Jinnie said. \"He's using the part of his brain you don't want to use, Mr. Poirot. Please, just think again, please. I would like to hear you say I didn't murder Richard Nigg before I'm hanged.\" [2482] I stood up and said: \"Let's go, Poirot. I wanted to end this conversation while the echo of 'hope' still lingered in the room.\" [2483] Epilogue\n[2484] Four days later, I sat by the fireplace in Mrs. Blanche Onsworth's house, sipping a glass of brandy and engrossed in a crossword puzzle when Poirot entered the living room and stood silently beside me for several minutes. I didn't look up at him. [2485] Finally, he cleared his throat and said: \"Caderpillar, you still won't talk to me about how Richard Nigg died - whether he was murdered, committed suicide with assistance, or killed in self-defense?\" [2486] \"I don't see the point in arguing about it,\" I said, feeling a spasm in my stomach. I no longer wanted to discuss the murder at the Baskerville Hotel. What I wanted to do, or needed to do, was to write it all down, to put the whole story on paper in detail. Was there such a difference between saying something and writing it down? [2487] \"Don't be afraid, my friend,\" said Poirot. \"I won't mention it again. We can talk about something else, like how I went to the 'Jolly Coffee Shop' this morning. Miss Spence asked me to tell you that she'd like to talk to you when you're free. She's a bit upset.\" [2488] \"With me?\" [2489] \"Yes. She said she was just hearing about the case at the Baskerville Hotel one moment, and the next, it was all over. A murder happened right in front of everyone, and yet no one saw the end. Miss Spence wants you to tell her the whole story from beginning to end.\" [2490] \"It wasn't my fault that murder happened,\" I whispered. \"Why can't she read about it in the newspaper like everyone else?\" [2491] \"No, she wants to talk to you alone. As a waitress, she's quite intelligent and a rare woman. Don't you think so, my friend?\" [2492] \"I know what you're up to, Poirot,\" I said weakly. \"Just drop it, stop wasting time, and tell Phyllida... sorry... you see, I'm busy. Can you leave?\" [2493] \"You're angry with me.\" [2494] \"Yes, a bit,\" I admitted. \"Henry Nayes and the suitcase, Raffles Burbank and the laundry cart, Thomas Brine and his girlfriend in the hotel garden, who was wearing a light brown coat, half of England wears such coats. And the handcart\u2026\"\n[2495] \"Ah!\" [2496] \"Yes, 'Ah'. You know that Jeanne Hobby isn't dead, yet you deliberately mislead me, making me suspect that her body was moved from Room 402, in the most impractical ways?\" [2497] \"My friend, because I wanted to encourage you to use your imagination. If you don't consider the most unlikely possibilities, you can't become the best detective. I'm teaching you to use your grey cells, forcing them to move in a different direction, so that you can be inspired.\" [2498] \"Do as you please.\" [2499] \"You must think that Poirot is going too far, beyond what is necessary. Maybe so.\" [2500] \"In Room 402, you made a fuss over those bloodstains, the direction of the bloodstains, the width of the door... What does it all mean? You know that Jeanne Hobby wasn't killed and wasn't dragged anywhere.\" [2501] \"I know, but you don't. You and our friend Mr. Lazari both believe that Jeanie is dead and that the blood on the floor belongs to her. Oh, I want you to think for yourself: a suitcase, a wheeled laundry cart - these two tools can directly enter Room 402 and be placed next to the body. So why did the killer drag the body towards the door? He couldn't have done that! Therefore, she couldn't have been dead! The bloodstains dragged towards the door were a deception, intended to make us believe that the body was moved elsewhere. This detail was done very convincingly, so it was very important as evidence at the crime scene. [2502] \"But for Hercule Poirot, it was just a small detail that allowed him to verify his suspicions. Jeanie Hobbs was not killed in that room, and no one was killed. Because I can't think of a reason to drag a bloody body to the door. No killer would walk around the hotel's public corridor with a victim's body, unless they put it in a container. But I can think of containers that can easily be brought into the room and directly put the body in and take it away, without dragging the body to the door. Caderpillar, this logic is very simple. I'm surprised you didn't think of it immediately.\" [2503] \"It's simple for you.\" I said, \"Next time, if you want me to think of something immediately, just tell me directly. Whatever it is, be direct, and it will save a lot of trouble.\" [2504] He smiled. \"Okay. I will learn to be more direct from my good friend Caderpillar. I will start learning now!\" [2505] \"If by 'private affairs' you mean Phyllis Spoon, I can tell you that we are impossible, forever impossible.\" I glanced at the letter in his hand. \"What about the private affairs of another pitiful person? Why thank you?\" [2506] Poirot smiled. \"Dr. Ambrose Flavard and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter. [2507] \"Who came to the letter?\u201d\n[2508] Poirot smiled. \"Ambrose Flavard, M.D., and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nOur attention was completely drawn to the room itself, which was so lavish that I couldn't take my eyes off it. I stood at the door, looking up at the high ceiling, which was decorated with intricate patterns and designs. It was hard to imagine people sitting under such a masterpiece, eating bread and jam, perhaps not even glancing up as they peeled boiled eggs. [422] I was trying to make sense of the complex design of the entire piece, wondering what relationship existed between the different patterns on the ceiling, when Luca Lazari rushed towards me. He spoke to me in a quivering voice, interrupting my appreciation of the artistic masterpiece above me. \"Mr. Kitchpur, Mr. Polo, I sincerely apologize! I was eager to assist you with such an important task, but I misinterpreted the message! I heard too many rumors and wanted to collect them, but I didn't do it properly. It's all my fault! No one else is to blame. Oh\u2026\"\n[423] Lazari stopped, looking back at the hundred or so men and women gathered in the restaurant. He then turned to his left, facing Polo, puffing out his chest and hanging his hands on either side of his hips, making a comical gesture. I think he was trying to shield his entire staff from Polo's distrustful gaze, believing that as long as Polo couldn't see them, he couldn't blame them. [424] \"Mr. Lazari, what mistake did you make?\" Polo asked. [425] \"A major mistake! You're right, it's impossible. But I hope you understand that the employees standing in front of you are all excellent, and they told me the truth. It was me who distorted the facts and misled you, but it wasn't intentional!\" [426] A short, stout, balding man stood up from the table. He appeared to be around fifty years old, resembling a Jew with his sad eyes, which made him look like a short-eared hound. \"It was me, sir,\" he said. [427] The \"excellent\" employees sat quietly around the table, attentively listening to every word. The atmosphere was tense. I quickly scanned their faces, but saw no smiles. [428] \"I previously told you that the three victims requested their dinner to be served in their respective rooms at 7:15 PM \u2013 separately,\" Lazari said. [429] \"Good. That makes sense. Who was the server you mentioned?\" Polo seemed pleased. [430] A short, stout, balding man stood up from the table. He appeared to be around fifty years old, resembling a Jew with his sad eyes, which made him look like a short-eared hound. \"It was me, sir,\" he said. [431] \"What is your name, sir?\" [432] \"Raphael Bork, sir.\" [433] \"Last night at 7:15, did you deliver the tea to Room 317 to Harriet Sipper, Edith Gainsborough, and Richard Nigges?\" Poirot asked him. [434] \"Not dinner, sir,\" Raphael Bork said. \"Tea, Mr. Nigges ordered. Dinner-time tea. He asked me if it was okay, if he had to order 'the main course' - he used that word. He told me that he and his friends didn't have an appetite for dinner, so they would have tea. I told him, sir, that you can order whatever you want, so he ordered three sandwiches with ham, cheese, smoked salmon, and cucumber, as well as a mixed cake set. And scones, sir, with jam and cream.\" [435] \"What did they drink?\" Poirot asked. [436] \"Tea, sir. All three of them wanted tea.\" [437] \"Oh. Did Mr. Nixey not order sherry?\" [438] Rafael Boccoli shook his head. \"No, sir, no sherry. Mr. Nixey did not order sherry, and I have not delivered any sherry to room 317.\" [439] \"Are you sure?\" [440] \"Absolutely, sir.\" [441] Feeling somewhat embarrassed under the scrutiny of so many eyes and realizing I had not asked a single question, I decided to intervene. \"Has anyone here delivered tea to room 121, where Miss Harriet Sipple resides, or sherry to Mr. Richard Nigges' room, either yesterday or the day before, on Wednesday?\" [442] All heads shook. It seemed that only Raphael Burbak had delivered anything to the rooms of the three victims, bringing \"evening tea\" to room 317 at 7:15 p.m. on Thursday, unless someone was lying. [443] I tried to make sense of the situation: The tea cup in Miss Harriet Sipple's room was fine, so it must have been one of the cups Burbak delivered, as only two remained after the incident. However, since no one had delivered sherry, how did the sherry glass end up in Mr. Richard Nigges' room? [444] Could the killer have come to the hotel with poison, a monogrammed cufflink, and a bottle of Harvey's sweet sherry in his pocket? That seemed far-fetched. [445] Burbak seemed equally concerned. \"Let me confirm, none of you delivered a glass of sherry to Mr. Richard Nigges, either to his room or anywhere else in the hotel?\" [446] \"Mr. Lazarus, is the glass in Mr. Nigg's room a Brouksen Hotel glass?\" [447] \"Yes, it is, Poirot. This is indeed strange. I suppose the server who delivered the wine to Mr. Nigg on Thursday or Wednesday didn't come today, but the staff on those days are here.\" [448] \"Indeed, that is strange,\" Poirot agreed. \"Mr. Buccleuch, when you delivered the 'evening tea' to Miss Adah Gransbury's room, what happened?\" [449] \"I put the items on the table and left, sir.\" [450] \"I see,\" Poirot said. \"Thank you for your cooperation.\" [451] \"Were all three of them in the room, Mrs. Westbury, Miss Garnesbury, and Mr. Nigges?\" [452] \"Yes, sir, they were all there.\" [453] \"Could you describe the situation?\" [454] \"Situation, sir?\" [455] Seeing Mr. Buccleuch's bewildered expression, I interjected, \"Who opened the door for you?\" [456] \"Mr. Nix let me in, sir.\" [457] \"Where are the two ladies?\" I asked. [458] \"Oh, they are sitting on the chairs by the fireplace, chatting. I only spoke to Mr. Nix, not to them. Sir, I put the things on the table by the window and left.\" [459] \"Can you recall what the two ladies were talking about?\" Poirot asked. [460] Burbank lowered his head: \"Oh, sir\u2026\"\n[461] \"This is important, sir. Every detail you can tell us about these three people is important.\" [462] \"Oh\u2026 they were a bit sneaky, sir. They were laughing, too.\" [463] \"Do you mean they were up to no good? How so?\" [464] \"One of them was. Mr. Nixes seemed quite happy. They were talking about an old woman and a young man. Since it was none of my business, I didn't pay much attention.\" [465] \"Do you remember anything specific they said? Who were they talking about?\" [466] \"Sorry, sir, I can't recall. I got the feeling they were talking about an old woman trying to woo a young man, just some gossip, you know.\" [467] \"Sir,\" said Poirot in the most serious tone, \"If you happen to remember any of their conversation \u2013 any at all \u2013 please tell me immediately.\" [468] \"I will, sir. Let me think... It was something like the young man left the old woman and ran off with another woman. Just some boring gossip, that's all.\" [469] \"So...,\" said Poirot, pacing around the room. As he moved, the heads of the hundred and twenty people followed his steps, turning with him as he turned. It was a curious sight. \"Richard Nigges, Harriet Sippele, and Ada Gransbury, a man and two women, whispering about another man and two women in Room 317!\" [470] \"What does it mean, Poirot?\" I asked. [471] \"Probably nothing, but it's interesting. Gossip, laughter, afternoon tea... It means our three victims were not strangers, but friends, and didn't realize they were about to meet their doom.\" [472] Suddenly, I jumped. A young man with black hair and a pale face, sitting at the table in front of me and Poirot, jumped up as if someone had pushed him from below. If it weren't for the terrified expression on his face, I would have thought he was eager to say something. [473] \"This is our new employee, Thomas Breenell, sir,\" Lazari waved at the young man, introducing him to us. [474] \"They're not just acquaintances, sir,\" Breenell spoke after a moment of silence, his voice barely audible, so that even those sitting behind him might not have heard what he was saying, \"They're good friends, very familiar with each other.\" [475] \"They're certainly good friends!\" Lazari exclaimed to the whole room, \"They even eat together!\" [476] \"Many people have to eat with people they don't like every day,\" Polo said. \"Mr. Brineau, please continue.\" [477] \"Last night, I met Mr. Niggles and he was very concerned about the two ladies. Only good friends would act that way,\" Thomas Brineau whispered. [478] \"Did you meet him?\" I asked, \"When? Where?\" [479] \"At 7:30, sir.\" He pointed to the double doors leading to the dining room, and I noticed his arm trembling. \"I was leaving when I saw him heading towards the elevator. He stopped when he saw me and called me over. I thought he was going back to his room.\" [480] \"What did he say?\" Polo asked. [481] \"He...he asked if the dinner bill was charged to his account, to make sure it wasn't charged to those two ladies. He said he had the money and could pay, but Mrs. Westphal and Miss Gransbury couldn't.\" [482] \"Did he say anything else, sir?\" Poirot asked. [483] \"No,\" Burrnell said, as if he would faint if he spoke another word. [484] \"Thank you, Mr. Burrnell,\" I said in a very gentle tone. \"Your information is very helpful. Thank you, Mr. Burbak, and thank everyone else too.\" [485] \"Carterpillar,\" Poirot murmured. \"Most people in the restaurant haven't spoken yet.\" [486] \"They've been listening and considering the matter, and we should be grateful for their attention.\" [487] \"You seem to trust them a lot, don't you? If we have any objections, should we gather all these people again? Let's ask these people... Mr. Burbank turned to the crowd, \"Ladies and gentlemen, we've all heard that Richard Nigg, Harry Voleur, and Edith Gransberry are friends. At seven-fifteen, their dinner was delivered to Room 317. But at seven-thirty, Mr. Burrnell saw Richard Nigg heading towards the elevator. He must have been going back to his room, right? To his own room, 238, or to Room 317 to visit his friends? And where did he come from? His sandwich and cake were delivered just fifteen minutes ago! Did he leave without eating? Or did he only spend a few minutes eating his own portion and then rushed out? Where did he rush off to? What urgent matter required him to leave Room 317? Was it to ensure that the dinner bill was not charged to Harry Voleur or Edith Gransberry's account? He didn't even wait for twenty or thirty minutes or an hour to do this?\" [488] At this point, a robust, brunette woman with curly hair stood up from the back of the restaurant, her forehead furrowed. \"You've asked so many questions all at once, as if I know, as if we all know. But we don't know anything!\" She spoke to Burbank, but looked around the restaurant at everyone. \"I want to go home, Mr. Lazari,\" she whimpered. \"I want to go check on my children to see if they're safe!\" [489] A young woman sitting next to her grabbed her arm, trying to calm her down. \"Sit down, Theresa,\" she said. \"That man just wants to help us. As long as your children aren't near the Brawnsham Hotel, they won't be harmed.\" [490] These soothing words caused both Lazari and Theresa Studd to groan in pain. [491] \"We won't keep you here for long, ma'am,\" I said, \"I promise that Mr. Lazari will let you go home to see your children as soon as we're done. \" Lazari immediately agreed, and Theresa calmed down and became more relaxed. [492] I turned to Pollock and said, \"Richard Nix left Room 317 not to handle the bill issue. He went somewhere else to handle his own business, met Thomas Breen on his way back, and decided to handle the bill issue. I hope this explanation will help everyone in the restaurant understand that we have questions, but we also have answers. Although not all questions have answers at the moment, having answers is better than having none.\" [493] \"Mr. Breen, do you remember the situation when you met Mr. Nix? Was it like Mr. Kacherpohl said, just a coincidence? He didn't come to find you on purpose, did he? On Tuesday, when he checked into the hotel, was it you who greeted him?\" [494] \"You're right, sir. He didn't come to find me on purpose.\" Breen sat in the chair, looking more comfortable, \"He happened to meet me, probably thinking, 'Oh, it's that guy again,' if you understand what I mean.\" [495]\n[496] \"Ladies and gentlemen, that's exactly what happened,\" Poirot said, raising his voice, \"Last night, after the crime, the killer, or someone who knows the killer and decides to help him commit the crime, left a note at the front desk, saying, 'May they rest in peace, 121, 238, 317.' Did anyone happen to see the person who left the note? It's this white piece of paper. Poirot took out the small white piece of paper from his pocket and held it high in the air, \"It was discovered by Mr. John Good at 8:10 p.m. last night. Did anyone notice that at that time, one or more people approached the front desk and behaved abnormally? Think hard! Someone must have seen something!\" [497] Theresa, who had closed her eyes and leaned on her friend, was silent. The restaurant was filled with whispers and gasps as everyone saw the killer's message, which made the triple murder case more realistic. [498] No one provided any more information. It turned out that if you asked a hundred people, you would only be disappointed. [499] Chapter 6\n[500] The Mystery of the Sherry\n[501] Thirty minutes later, Polo and I sat in the \"secret lounge\" mentioned by Lazare, sipping coffee and warming ourselves by the fire, which burned brightly. The \"secret lounge\" was hidden behind the restaurant, not connected to any public corridors. The walls around the lounge were adorned with portraits, which I tried my best not to look at. I would have appreciated a pleasant landscape painting, even a gloomy one. The eyes in the portraits always made me uncomfortable, no matter which artist had painted them. I always felt like the people in the portraits were mocking me, and I couldn't escape their mocking gaze. [502] Just moments ago, Bolo was still passionately delivering a speech like a program host in the restaurant, but now he seems to be feeling down again. \"Are you worrying about Jenny again?\" I asked him. [503] He admitted, \"I don't want to hear news that there's a cufflink in her mouth with the initials PIJ engraved on it. I'm afraid to hear such news.\" [504] \"Considering that you can't do anything right now, I suggest you think about something else.\" I advised him. [505] \"Hello, reality, Kagipur. Alright, let's think about the tea cup then.\" [506] \"Teacups?\" [507] \"Yes. What do you think?\" [508] I thought for a moment and said, \"I don't have any opinion on teacups.\" [509] Polo made an impatient sound. \"Rafael Bacchus sent three teacups to Ada Gransbury's room. Three people, three teacups, that makes sense. But when they found their bodies, there were only two teacups in the room.\" [510] \"The other one was in Harriet Sippler's room, with her body,\" I said. [511] \"Correct, but that's also the most curious part, isn't it? Did Mrs. Spear carry the teacups and saucers back to her room? Would she carry a cup of tea through the hotel corridors, then take the elevator or climb two flights of stairs, regardless of whether there was poison in the tea? If the teacup was full, the tea could spill; if it was half-full or almost empty, there would be no need to carry it. Generally speaking, people drink tea in the room where it's served, don't they?\" [512] \"Generally speaking, yes. But this murderer is clearly far from ordinary, and he shocks me greatly.\" [513] \"And the victims? Are they ordinary people? Are their behaviors normal? Are you trying to make me believe that Harriet Spear carried the teacups back to her room, sat down in a chair, and was about to drink her tea when the murderer knocked on the door and took the opportunity to put cyanide in her tea? And don't forget Richard Nigges. For some reason, he left Edith Gransberry's room but quickly returned to his own room, holding a glass of sherry. But no one had sent him sherry at the hotel.\" [514] \"I believe\u2026\" I said. [515] But Poirot continued, as if he hadn't heard me. \"Ah, yes, Richard Nigges too. The murderer went to his room to find him, and he was sitting there alone, holding a drink, as if saying to the murderer, 'Please put the poison in my drink.' At that moment, was Edith Gransberry alone in Room 317, patiently waiting for the murderer? Sipping her tea slowly, it would be inconsiderate to finish it before the murderer arrived. How could the murderer put cyanide in it then? Where would he put the cyanide?\" [516] \"Damn it, Poirot, what do you want me to say? I know as much as you do! Listen, I just think that these three victims must have argued.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe plan she described was the same as the one she had told me and Poirot about at Samuel Kid's house, including how things had gone awry later. When she mentioned that they would frame Nancy Duane with the murders, ensuring she would be hanged, Louisa Wallace let out a shriek. \"Framing an innocent woman, putting her to death for three murders she did not commit, is not correcting the mistake! This is evil!\" exclaimed St. John Wallace. [2172] No one contradicted him, at least no one spoke out against him. I noticed that Phyllis Spurling did not seem as shocked as most people, and she appeared to be listening intently. [2173] \"I never intended to frame Nancy,\" said Jenny. \"Never! Whether you believe me or not, it doesn't matter.\" [2174] \"Mr. Niggles,\" said Poirot, \"Henry Niggles, do you think your brother Richard would devise such a plan?\" [2175] Henry Niggles stood up and said, \"I don't think so, Mr. Poirot. The Richard I know would never think of killing anyone, of course, the Richard who moved to Devonshire and lived with me sixteen years ago is not the same as the Richard I know. Oh, they look alike on the outside, but they are completely different on the inside. I'm afraid I never really understood the one who came after, so I can't really comment on whether he might have done such a thing.\" [2176] \"Thank you, Mr. Nigges. And thank you, Miss Hobbs.\" He added coldly, \"Please sit down.\" [2177] He turned to the crowd and said, \"Ladies and gentlemen, you see, if Miss Hobbs' words are true, we don't need to arrest anyone, no one is guilty. Adah Gransberry killed her with the permission of Harriet Sipper, Richard Nigges killed her with the permission of Adah Gransberry, and then, since Jenny Hobbs did not go to the hotel as planned, Richard Nigges committed suicide. He locked the door, hid the key behind a loose tile in the fireplace, opened the window, and ended his life, making the scene look like a murder. The police would think that the killer, Nancy Drew, escaped with the key, using the open window and the tree by the window. But according to Jenny Hobbs, there was no killer, everyone died willingly!\" [2178] Poirot looked around the dining room. \"There is no killer,\" he repeated. \"Nevertheless, there are still two living criminals who should be punished. They are Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid, who conspired to frame Nancy Drew.\" [2179] \"I hope you lock them up, Mr. Poirot!\" said Louisa Wallace loudly. [2180] \"I don't have the authority to open or close the prison gates, madam. That's the work of my friend Cader and his colleagues. I'm just responsible for uncovering the hidden and the truth. Mr. Samuel Kid, please stand up.\" [2181] Kid stood up, looking uncomfortable. [2182] \"Your task in this plan was to place a note at the hotel's reception desk, was it not? The note read, 'May they rest in peace, 121, 238, 317'?\" [2183] \"Yes, sir. Just as Miss Jennie said, that's exactly what it was.\" [2184] \"Did Miss Jennie give you the note?\" [2185] \"Yes, she gave it to me earlier that day. That morning.\" [2186] \"When did you put it on the table?\" [2187] \"Shortly after eight o'clock in the evening, just like Jenny said. The sooner after eight, the better. But first, make sure no one is around, no one will see me.\" [2188] \"Who said that?\" Polo asked. [2189] \"Jenny.\" [2190] \"Is it Jenny who told you to put the key in Nancy Duane's pocket?\" [2191] \"Yes.\" Kid spoke with annoyance. \"She just said it, and I don't understand why you're asking me again.\" [2192] \"Alright, let me explain. According to Jenny Hobson's original plan, Jenny was supposed to give all three room keys to Samuel Kid after killing Richard, and Samuel would then find a way to plant the keys in a place that would frame Nancy Drew - which, as it turned out, was in Nancy Drew's coat pocket. However, Jenny Hobson said that she didn't go to the Blaukhan Hotel that night. She didn't have the courage to do so. So, I'd like to ask you, Mr. Kid, how did you get the keys to rooms 121 and 317?\" [2193] \"How did I... how did I get those keys?\" [2194] \"Yes, that's my question. Please answer.\" [2195] \"I... Well, if you must know, I got the keys through my own cleverness. I spoke nicely to an employee at the hotel and asked if he could do me a favor and let me use the master key, and he agreed. I returned the key after I was done, and the whole process was very discreet.\" [2196] I was close to Poirot and could hear him make an annoyed sound. He said, \"Which employee, sir? All the employees are here. Point out the one who gave you the master key.\" [2197] \"I can't remember who it was. It was a man. I can't remember people's faces.\" [2198] \"So, you used the master key to enter three rooms?\" [2199] \"No, I only went to room 238. All the keys should have been there, waiting for Jenny to come and take them, but I only found two. As you said, there's another one hidden behind a loose tile in the fireplace, but Mr. Nigel's body was lying there, and I didn't want to stay there too long, so I didn't look for the third key in the room.\" [2200] \"You're lying,\" Poirot said to him. \"It doesn't matter. You'll find that lying won't help you get out of this predicament. Let's continue. No, don't sit down yet. I have another question for you and Jenny Hobbs. You two planned that, after 7:30 p.m. on the night of the murder, Jenny would come to the 'Joyful Coffee House' and tell me the story she had prepared about how scared she was of being murdered, right?\" [2201] \"Yes.\" Jenny answered, looking at Samuel Kid instead of Poirot. [2202] \"I'm sorry, I still have an important question that I haven't figured out. You said you were too scared to carry out the plan, so you didn't go to the hotel at 6 p.m. But it seems that the plan went smoothly without you, the only difference being that Richard Nigges committed suicide, not by you putting poison in his drink. Lady, am I right?\" [2203] \"Yes, that's right.\" [2204] \"So, the only detail that changed was that Richard Nigges committed suicide, so we can say that the death still occurred according to plan: between 7:15 p.m. and 8:00 p.m., after ordering sandwiches and scones. Right, Ms. Hobbs?\" [2205] \"Yes.\" Jenny said, but her voice didn't sound as confident as before. [2206] \"So, I would like to ask, how could you possibly carry out the plan to kill Richard Nigges? You told us that you planned to meet me at 'The Happy Cafe' after seven-thirty, knowing that I always go there for dinner on Thursdays. But from the Bluxton Hotel to 'The Happy Cafe', it takes at least half an hour, no matter what mode of transportation you use. So, even if you killed Adah Gransbury quickly at seven-fifteen and Richard Nigges quickly killed Adah Gransbury, you still wouldn't have been able to be at the 238 room to kill Richard Nigges and then make it to the cafe by seven-thirty. Do you want us to believe that, in all your careful planning of this action, you didn't consider that it was actually impossible?\" [2207] Jenny's face went pale, and I felt mine do the same, even though I couldn't see it. [2208] Poirot's pointed out flaw was so obvious that I hadn't noticed it. I hadn't even thought of it. [2209] Chapter XXIII\n[2210] The True Adah Gransbury\n[2211] Samuel Kidd chuckled softly and turned to face the others in the restaurant. \"Mr. Poirot, you take pride in your investigative abilities, but it seems your powers of observation are not as sharp as you claim. I can say with confidence that I am more familiar with this plan than you are, as Jane has mentioned it countless times. The original plan was not to start at seven fifteen, but at six o'clock. I don't know where these ideas came from. Asking for food at seven fifteen was not part of the plan.\" [2212] \"Yes,\" Jane said, her sharp mind finding a way out of the trap that seemed to have restored her composure. \"I can only say that I was late to the hotel at six o'clock, which caused the plan to be delayed. Others may have wanted to discuss why I didn't show up. I should have been there by then. The discussion would have taken some time.\" [2213] \"Ah, of course. But a few minutes ago, when I asked you if the deaths were carried out according to the agreed plan, between seven fifteen and eight o'clock, you didn't correct me. And neither of you mentioned that the afternoon tea was not part of the plan.\" [2214] \"I apologize for not correcting your statement earlier,\" Jane said. \"I... I mean, it's so oppressive here.\" [2215] \"So, the three executions were scheduled to begin at six o'clock, then?\" [2216] \"Yes, everything was supposed to be completed by 6:45 so that I could arrive at 'The Happy Coffee Shop' at 7:30.\" [2217] \"Then I have another question for you, madam. Why, in your plan, was Mr. Kid supposed to wait an hour before placing the note on the hotel's front desk? By then, Harriet, Ada, and Richard were already dead, and you had left the hotel. Why not at another time, such as 7:15 or 7:30? Why choose 8:00?\" [2218] Jenny recoiled as if struck on the head. \"Why not 8:00?\" she challenged. \"Waiting a little longer won't hurt. [2219] \"Your question is ridiculous, Mr. Poirot,\" said Samuel Kid. [2220] \"Waiting a little longer won't hurt, madam. I agree. So we must ask, what was the purpose of leaving the note? Why not wait until the next morning, when the hotel staff would naturally discover the three bodies? Jenny, don't look at Samuel Kid, look at me, Hercule Poirot! Answer my question.\" [2221] \"I...I don't know! I think maybe Richard...\"\n[2222] \"No! Not Richard!\" Poirot said to her. \"If you don't want to answer my question, I'll answer it for you. You made Mr. Kid put the note on the front desk just after 8:00 because the murder was planned to be done between 7:15 and 8:00!\" [2223] Poirot turned around and faced the crowd with widened eyes, listening quietly. \"Let's think about the afternoon tea that the three victims ordered and delivered to Room 317, Ada Grantham's room. Let's imagine that the three people who willingly sought death were confused because Jane didn't show up. They didn't know what to do, so they went to Ada Grantham's room to discuss it. Mr. Cargill, would you order scones and cakes if you were about to be executed for your past sins?\" [2224] \"No, I would be too nervous to eat or drink.\" [2225] \"Perhaps our three executioners thought they needed to conserve their energy for the upcoming task,\" Poirot speculated. \"However, when the food arrived, they couldn't eat it. But where did the food go?\" [2226] \"Are you asking me?\" Jennie said, \"I don't know, because I wasn't there.\" [2227] \"Let's go back to the time of the murder,\" Poirot said. \"The pathologist determined that the three deaths occurred between four and eight-thirty. Later, indirect evidence narrowed the time of death to seven-fifteen to eight-ten. Alright, let's look at these indirect evidence. Server Rafael Bocar was in Room 317 at seven-fifteen and saw the three victims, and then Thomas Braynell met Richard Nigges in the hotel lobby at seven-thirty, where Nigges praised Braynell's efficiency and asked him to ensure the tea point charges were on his account, and ordered a sherry. So, none of the cases happened before seven-fifteen, and Richard Nigges couldn't have died before seven-thirty. [2228] \"However, there are still many details that don't fit together, and we can't form a complete picture. First, we know that Harriet Sipper, Adela Granbriery, and Richard Nigges didn't eat those pastries, but they disappeared. I don't believe that a person who is about to commit their first murder would want to eat a scone before doing so. Since they didn't plan to eat, why did they order them? It can only be to create an alibi, to prove that they were still alive at seven-fifteen. But why make people think that the three victims were still alive at that time? According to Jennie Hobbs, I can only think of one explanation: if our conspirators knew, for whatever reason, that Nancy Duane had no alibi between seven-fifteen and eight-fifteen, then they had to ensure that the deaths occurred within that time frame. But Nancy Duane's alibi was very solid, wasn't it, Mrs. Wallace?\" [2229] Louisa Wallace stood up and said, \"Yes, she had one. She ate dinner at my house, and then stayed with me and my husband until about ten o'clock in the evening.\" [2230] \"Thank you, ma'am. So, why did they have to make it look like the murders happened between seven-fifteen and eight-ten, I can only think of one reason. That is, during that time, Jennie Hobbs also had an unbreakable alibi. That is, I, Hercule Poirot, can perfectly prove that she was not at the Brooks Han Hotel at that time. She was with me from seven-thirty-five to seven-fifty in 'Joy Cafe'. Additionally, I have already mentioned the distance between the two places.\" [2231] \"I put these evidences together, along with my belief that the three people did not die between 7:15 PM and 8:10 PM. So I thought, why go to such lengths to prove that Jane did not commit the murder? Unless, she really did it.\" [2232] Jane jumped up from her chair, saying, \"I didn't kill them! I swear I didn't! And they must have died between 7:15 PM and 8:00 PM, everyone here knows it except you!\" [2233] \"Sit down and shut up, Miss Hobbs,\" said Poirot coldly. [2234] Samuel Kidd's face was full of anger. \"These are all made up, Mr. Poirot! How do you know they didn't order the food because they were hungry?\" [2235] \"Well, Mr. Kidd, why didn't they eat them?\" I asked. \"Where did the sandwiches and cakes go?\" [2236] \"That was the best afternoon tea in London!\" murmured Luca Lazari. [2237] \"I'll tell you where they went, Caderpohl,\" said Poirot. \"Our murderer made a mistake - just one of many. If there had been food on the plate in Room 317, it would have been fine. The police would have thought that the killer came in before they had a chance to enjoy their snacks. But the killer was afraid that leaving food would arouse suspicion, he didn't want anyone to ask, 'Why didn't you eat the tea and cake?'\" [2238] \"What happened to the food?\" I asked. \"Where did it go?\" [2239] \"The conspirators took the food away from the scene. Yes, ladies and gentlemen, there was a plot and three murders were committed! There's one more thing I haven't explained - Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nigges were already dead before 7:15.\" [2240] Hearing this, Luca Lazari stepped forward and said, \"Mr. Poirot, I must interrupt you. I must tell you that Raphael Bukar is our most loyal employee and he would not lie. He saw the three victims alive and well when he went to deliver the food at 7:15. Alive and well! You must be mistaken.\" [2241] \"I didn't. But one thing you're right about, Raffaello Puccini is a good witness. He saw three people in Room 317 when he delivered the tea, but they weren't Harriet Spear, Edith Gransbury, and Richard Nix.\" [2242] Everyone in the restaurant gasped in shock, and I was no exception, trying to figure out who the three people could be. Not Janice Hobbs, who was on her way to \"The Happy Cafe.\" Who could it be? [2243] \"Poirot,\" I said nervously, \"You mean there were three people dressed up as the victims just to make the waiter think they were still alive when he delivered the tea?\" [2244] \"No, that's not quite it. In fact, two people dressed up as two of the victims. The third person, Edith Gransbury... unfortunately, she wasn't a fake. No, unfortunately, she was the real Edith Gransbury. Mr. Puccini, do you remember how you described what you saw when you delivered the tea to Room 317? You told me twice, and I remember every word. For everyone's sake, don't mind if I repeat it?\" [2245] \"No, sir, I don't mind.\" [2246] \"Thank you. You found the three victims alive - of course, they were talking about a mutual acquaintance. You heard Harriet Sipple, or the woman referred to as 'Harriet' in the room say, 'She has no other choice, does she? He no longer trusts her. He doesn't like her now, and it's her own choice to go there, and she's old enough to be his mother. No, if she wants to know what he's thinking, she has no choice but to accept the woman he trusts and talk to her.' At that point, the man in the room ignored you and the food and said, 'Oh, Harriet, that's not fair. Ada can be easily frightened, be kind to her.' Do I have it right, Mr. Burbank?\" [2247] \"Yes, sir.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nNancy Drew was very beautiful, with a face as pink as a peach, black and shiny hair, and deep blue eyes under long lashes. She looked to be around forty years old, wearing a fashionable dress in shades of peacock blue and deep green. For the first time, Poirot was not the most elegantly dressed person in the room. [1259] \"Mrs. Drew, it's a pleasure to meet you. I admire your artistic talent, and I've had the honor of seeing a few of your works at exhibitions recently. You have a truly precious gift.\" [1260] \"Thank you. You flatter me. Now, let's take off your coat and hat, and we'll find a comfortable place to sit and chat. Would you like some tea or coffee?\" [1261] \"Yes, thank you.\" [1262] \"Great. Please follow me.\" [1263] They went to a small living room, and I'm glad I only have to listen to the description later, as Poirot described the room to be full of portraits. The walls were covered with watchful eyes...\n[1264] Poirot asked Miss Nancy Duane if all the portraits on the walls were her paintings. [1265] \"Oh, no,\" she said, \"not many of them are mine. I buy as many paintings as I sell, and I think it's better. I love art.\" [1266] \"So do I,\" Poirot told her. [1267] \"It can be lonely to only appreciate your own work, and hard to bear. I often think of hanging another artist's work on the wall as hanging a good friend.\" [1268] \"I agree. You explained it very well, Madame.\" [1269] They had just sat down when Nancy said, \"Allow me to get straight to the point. What brings you here? You said on the phone that you wanted to search my home. You are certainly welcome to do so. But why?\" [1270] \"Madame, you may have seen in the papers that three guests at the Browns Hotel were killed last Thursday evening.\" [1271] \"At the Bluxton Hotel? \" Nancy smiled, then her face fell. \"Oh, dear, is that true? Three? You're sure? I always thought Bluxton was a high-class hotel, I can't imagine there would be murders there.\" [1272] \"So you know the hotel?\" [1273] \"Yes, I often go there for afternoon tea. Their manager, Lazari, is very likable. You know, their scones are famous, the best in London. Sorry... \" She paused, \"Three people were killed there, I shouldn't be talking about scones here. It's terrible. But I don't see how it's related to me.\" [1274] \"You didn't see the newspaper report?\" Polo asked. [1275] \"No.\" Nancy Dunn's lips were in a straight line. \"I don't read newspapers, and we don't subscribe to them. The newspapers are full of misfortunes. I don't want to see misfortunes.\" [1276] \"So you don't know the names of the three victims?\" [1277] \"No, I don't want to know.\" [1278] \"Well, whether you want to or not, I'm afraid I have to tell you. Their names are Harriet Sipper, Edith Gransbury, and Richard Nigg.\" [1279] \"Oh, no, no. Oh, Mr. Poirot! I'm not joking, am I? Tell me what's going on.\" [1280] \"This is not a joke, madam. I'm sorry to upset you.\" [1281] \"Hearing their names does upset me. Whether they're alive or dead, it doesn't matter to me. I don't intend to remember them. You see, the more you try to forget something, the more it sticks with you, and\u2026 I hate seeing misery more than most people.\" [1282] \"Have you experienced a lot of misery?\" [1283] \"I don't want to talk about personal matters.\" [1284] Poirot was in a difficult position, as he wanted to talk about everything she didn't want to talk about. But there was nothing he liked more than the personal matters of strangers he might never see again. [1285] He changed the subject and asked, \"Let's talk about the purpose of my visit, something related to the police investigation. Do you know the three victims?\" [1286] Nancy nodded. \"I used to live in a village called Gleeholm in Pigeon Valley. You wouldn't know the place, no one does. Harry, Ada, and Richard were my neighbors until I moved to London in 1913. I haven't seen or heard from them since. Were they killed?\" [1287] \"Yes, ma'am.\" [1288] \"At the Blauxhams Hotel? What were they doing there? Why did they come to London?\" [1289] \"That's one of the questions I wanted to ask,\" Poirot said to her. [1290] \"It makes no sense to kill them,\" Nancy said, standing up from her chair and walking back and forth between the door and the opposite wall. \"The only person who would do such a thing didn't do it!\" [1291] \"Who is that person?\" [1292] \"Oh, don't look at me,\" Nancy sat back down in her chair, \"Sorry. This news has shocked me. I can't help you. You see... I don't want to be impolite, but I would like you to leave now.\" [1293] \"Madam, you mean that only you could have killed these three people? But you didn't kill them, did you?\" [1294] \"I didn't...,\" Nancy looked around the room, slowly saying, \"Ah, now I understand why you came here. You heard some stories and thought I killed them, so you wanted to search my house. But I didn't kill anyone. However, to satisfy your curiosity, Monsieur Poirot, let Tabitha show you around every room. My house has many rooms, and you might miss a few if she doesn't show you.\" [1295] \"Thank you, madam.\" [1296] \"You won't find any evidence of a crime because there isn't any. I hope you leave soon! You have no idea how much I'm hurt.\" [1297] Stanley Bill stood up. \"Well, I'll begin. Thank you for your cooperation, Madam.\" He left the drawing room and closed the door. [1298] \"You are so clever, aren't you, Madam Dunn?\" Nancy said to Poirot, as if protesting. \"You are as clever as people say. I can see it in your eyes.\" [1299] \"People say I have an extraordinary brain. Yes, I do.\" [1300] \"You are so proud. In my opinion, an extraordinary brain is useless unless it is accompanied by an extraordinary heart.\" [1301] \"Of course, as a lover of great art, we must believe this. The heart and soul can perceive art better than the brain.\" [1302] \"I agree.\" Nancy said quietly, \"You know, Mr. Poirot, your eyes...they're not just clever. They're wise. They can connect to the distant past. Oh, you probably don't understand what I mean, but that's the truth. They must be beautiful in a painting, but I won't give you a portrait, especially with those three terrible names on my doorstep today.\" [1303] \"That's unfortunate.\" [1304] \"It's your fault.\" Nancy folded her hands, speaking honestly, \"Oh, I think I should tell you: what I just said was about myself. If someone wanted to kill Harriet, Ada, and Richard, it would be me, but I also said I didn't do it. So, I have no idea what happened.\" [1305] \"You don't like them?\" [1306] \"I hate them. I've wished them dead countless times. Oh, dear!\" [1307] \"Why do you dislike them so much?\" [1308] \"I don't want to talk about it.\" [1309] \"Madam, if I thought it was irrelevant, I wouldn't ask you.\" [1310] \"But I don't want to answer.\" [1311] Poirot sighed. \"Where were you between 7:15 and 8:00 p.m. last Thursday?\" [1312] Nancy frowned. \"I can't remember. Keeping track of what I should do each week is a real bother. Oh, wait. Thursday, yes, I went to my friend Louis's house across the street, Louis Wallace. I finished her portrait and delivered it, then stayed for dinner. I think I arrived at around 6:00 p.m. and stayed until about 10:00 p.m. I might have stayed longer if it weren't for her husband, Saint John. He's such a snob, and she's so sweet, never nitpicking about anyone's faults, you know the type. She insisted that Saint John and I would get along well, being artists, but I can't stand him. He claims his art is superior to mine and never misses an opportunity to show it off. He's obsessed with painting plants and fish, boring leaves or cold-eyed cod and haddock!\" [1313] \"Is he an animal or plant artist?\" [1314] \"I'm not interested in painters who don't paint people. Sorry, but that's just how it is. Saint John insists that every portrait must have a story, and once you start telling a story, you inevitably distort the visual expression, which is just nonsense! What's wrong with telling a story?\" [1315] \"Would Saint John Wallace confirm that you were with him from 6:00 p.m. to almost 10:00 p.m. on Thursday last week?\" Poirot asked her, \"Can he prove that you were at his house during that time?\" [1316] \"Of course he would. Poirot, that's ridiculous. You're questioning me like an accused murderer, but I'm not the murderer. Who told you those three people were killed by me?\" [1317] \"At eight o'clock on Thursday night, someone saw you come running out of the Blaukens Hotel, very agitated. You dropped two keys on the ground as you ran, picked them up, and ran off again. The person who saw this recognized you from the newspaper as the famous artist Nancy Drew. But that's impossible, isn't it?\" [1318] \"That's ridiculous. Your eyewitness must have made a mistake. Ask Saint John and Louisa Wallace.\" [1319] \"I will, madam. And one more question. Can you recognize the initials PIJ or PJI? It could be someone from Gleghorn Village.\" [1320] Nancy's color drained from her face. \"Yes,\" she whispered. \"Patrick James Ivy, a former clergyman.\" [1321] \"Ah! The priest, did he die a terrible death? Did his wife die a terrible death too?\" [1322] \"Yes.\" [1323] \"What happened to them?\" [1324] \"I don't want to talk about it. I don't want to!\" [1325] \"This is crucial, and I must insist that you tell me.\" [1326] \"I can't!\u201d exclaimed Nancy, \"even if I wanted to, I can't tell you. You can't understand. So long I haven't spoken of this, I\u2026\u201d She stammered for a long time, not getting out a word. Then her face twisted with pain. \"What happened to Harry, Edith, and Richard?\" she asked, \"How were they killed?\" [1327] \"Poisoned.\" [1328] \"Oh, how terrible! But that's just how it should be.\" [1329] \"Why, ma'am? Was it because Patrick Eves and his wife also died from poisoning?\" [1330] \"I told you, I can't talk about them!\" [1331] \"Do you know Jennie from Gleechol?\" [1332] Nancy gasped, putting her hand to her throat. \"Jennie Hobbs, I have nothing to say about her, not a word. Don't ask me any more questions!\" She wiped away her tears and said, \"How can people be so cruel, Mr. Poirot? Do you know? No, don't answer! Let's talk about something else, something cheerful.\" [1333] \"This is my father,\" Nancy said, \"Algernon Johnson. You may have heard of him.\" [1334] \"It rings a bell, but I can't quite place it.\" [1335] \"He passed away two years ago. The last time I saw him, I was nineteen, and now I'm forty-two.\" [1336] \"Please accept my condolences.\" [1337] \"It's not my painting. I don't know who painted it or when it was painted. There's no signature or date, so I think it's not from a professional artist, whoever he is, but an amateur. But... my father is smiling in the painting, so I bought it and hung it on the wall. If he could smile more in life... \" Nancy didn't finish her sentence, turning to face Poirot, \"Did you see that? Saint John was wrong! The duty of art is to replace unhappy facts with happy creations.\" [1338] At that moment, there was a loud knocking at the door, and Stanley Bill, the police officer, entered. He stared at Poirot, avoiding Nancy's gaze, and Poirot knew there was something wrong. \"I found something, sir,\" he said. [1339] \"What is it?\" [1340] \"Two keys, found in the pocket of a blue coat with a fur collar. The maid said it belonged to Mrs. Dunn.\" [1341] \"What keys?\" Nancy said, \"Let me see. I never put keys in my clothes pockets. I have a drawer for my keys.\" [1342] Bill still didn't look at her, walking to Poirot's chair and standing next to him, loosening his clenched fist. [1343] \"What did he have?\" Nancy asked impatiently. [1344] \"Two keys, with room numbers engraved on them, from the Bluxton Hotel.\" Poirot said solemnly, \"Room 121 and Room 317.\" [1345] \"What do these numbers have to do with me?\" Nancy asked. [1346] \"Madame, two murders took place in those two rooms: Room 121 and Room 317. The witness who saw you running out of the Blaxland Hotel on the night of the crime said he saw you drop two keys with numbers on them: 101 and 301.\" [1347] \"Why, that's too coincidental! Oh, Mr. Poirot! Do you really think you're so clever? Don't you see what you're up against? It's a conspiracy! I'm interested in finding out who's behind it \u2013 as long as we all agree that I shouldn't be sent to the gallows now.\" [1348] \"Who had the opportunity to put the keys in your coat pocket from last Thursday to now?\" Poirot asked her. [1349] \"How would I know? Anyone who walked past me on the street could have. I often wear that blue coat. You know, it's unreasonable.\" [1350] \"Explain that.\" [1351] For a moment, she seemed lost in thought, then she snapped out of it and said, \"Those who dislike Harriet, Ada, and Richard to the point of wanting to kill them... well, they should have some fondness for me. However, now they're trying to frame me for the murders.\" [1352] \"Should we arrest her, sir?\" Stanley Bill asked Poirot, \"Detain her?\" [1353] \"Oh, don't be ridiculous,\" Nancy said wearily, \"I just said 'someone's trying to frame me,' and you want to arrest me immediately? Are you a policeman or a parrot? If you want to arrest someone, arrest that witness. What if he's not only a liar but also the real culprit? Have you thought about that? You should go to the other side of the street and ask St. John and Louisa Wallace what's really going on. That's the only way to stop all this nonsense.\" [1354] Poirot struggled to stand up from the chair, which was not very comfortable for someone of his size. \"We will go. For now, there's no need to arrest anyone, Inspector. Madam, I believe if you had killed someone in rooms 121 and 317 at the Brawnsham Hotel, you would not have kept these keys. Why didn't you throw them away?\" [1355] \"That's right. I would have thrown them away immediately, wouldn't I?\" [1356] \"I'm going to visit the Wallace couple right now.\" [1357] \"To be precise,\" Nancy said, \"you should call them Sir John and Lady Wallace. Lady Wallace doesn't care, but if you ignore their titles, Saint John won't forgive you.\" [1358] Soon, Poirot stood next to Lady Wallace, who was gazing intently at a portrait on the wall, a portrait painted by Nancy Duane. \"Isn't it perfect?\" she breathed, \"Not flattery, not sarcasm. Rosy cheeks, round face, just like me. I was always worried she'd paint me as a farmer's wife, but she didn't. I don't look like a beauty queen, but I think I look nice. Saint John used a word he's never used to describe me before, 'charming,' and it's this painting that made him feel that way.\" She smiled. \"It's wonderful to have a genius like Nancy, isn't it?\" [1359] Poirot found it difficult to focus on the painting. Nancy Duane had a smart and efficient maid, but Lady Wallace's maid was clumsy. Her name was Dorka, and she had dropped Poirot's coat twice and his hat once, stepping on it in the process. [1360] In some ways, the Wallace household might have been quite beautiful. But that day, everything Poirot saw was beyond his tolerance. Apart from the heavy furniture properly placed against the walls, everything else seemed to have been blown by the wind, scattered haphazardly around the house, blocking his way. Poirot couldn't stand the chaos; it would hinder his clear thinking. [1361] Dorcas finally picked up Poirot's coat and the hat that had been trampled on and left the room, leaving Poirot and Louisa Wallace alone. Stanley Bill stayed at Nancy Drew's house to continue the search. Sir Wallace was not at home and had gone to the family estate early in the morning. Poirot did find a few paintings on the wall that Nancy called \"dull leaves or cold-eyed cod and blackspot cod.\" He suspected they might be Sir John Wallace's works. [1362] \"I apologize for Dorcas,\" said Louisa. \"She's new and doesn't know how to do anything yet, and it's causing us trouble. But I believe she'll get better. She's only been here for three days and needs time and patience to learn. If only she wasn't so tense! I know what's going on: she tells herself she can't let an important guest's coat and hat fall on the ground, and the thought of 'falling on the ground' gets stuck in her head, and that's exactly what happens. It's infuriating!\" [1363] \"Indeed,\" Poirot agreed.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThis Richard, who later realized that he should not condemn and persecute a person for a human weakness, would he do that? This Richard, who rigidly believed that all criminals should be severely punished, thus breaking off his engagement with Ada Gransbury, would he let Nancy Drew, an innocent woman who only mistakenly fell in love with a man who didn't belong to her, bear the burden of three lives and face the legal consequences? Pfft, nonsense! It doesn't make sense. This is Jenny Hobbs' delusion, to mislead us again.\" [1987] I listened to this speech with my mouth half open. \"Poirot, are you sure? I believe her.\" [1988] \"Of course I'm sure. Henry Nixes didn't tell us that his brother Richard Nixes has been living like a recluse at home for sixteen years, never seeing anyone and never speaking to anyone? But Jenny Hobbs says that he has been persuading Harriet Spear and Ada Gransbury for sixteen years to take responsibility for the deaths of Patrick and Francis Eves and to pay the price. If Richard Nixes had been regularly in contact with the two women from Gleghorn, how could his brother Henry Nixes not notice that?\" [1989] \"That's a good point, but I don't agree.\" [1990] \"That's not the point. You must have noticed that Jenny's story is fundamentally wrong.\" [1991] \"It is certainly wrong to frame an innocent person for murder.\" [1992] \"Carter, I'm not talking about moral 'wrongness', but about factual impossibility. Are you trying to provoke me into explaining it clearly? Okay, let me remind you of a detail that may help you understand everything. According to Janie Hobbs, how did the keys to rooms 121 and 317 of the Blaukshan Hotel end up in Nancy Drew's blue coat pocket?\" [1993] \"Samuel Kid put them there to frame Nancy.\" [1994] \"Did he slip them in on the street?\" [1995] \"I think it's easy.\" [1996] \"Yes, but how did Samuel Kid get those two keys? It should have been Jenny who found the keys, went to room 238 to kill Richard Nigg, and then left with three keys, locked the door, and then gave the keys to Samuel Kid. But according to Jenny, she didn't go to Richard Nigg's room that night, nor did she go to the Brooks Han Hotel. She said that Mr. Nigg locked the door from the inside, hid the keys behind a loose tile in the fireplace, and then committed suicide. So, how did Samuel Kid get those two keys?\" [1997] I waited for Poirot to continue, but he didn't. So I said, \"I don't know.\" [1998] \"Maybe because Jenny Hobbs didn't come, Samuel Kid and Richard Nigg came up with an idea: the former killed the latter and took the keys to rooms 121 and 317 from Richard Nigg's room? But why didn't he take the key to his own room? Why did he still hide it behind a loose tile in the fireplace? The only reasonable explanation is that Richard Nigg committed suicide, but he wanted his suicide to look like a murder. My friend, but it's easy to do, just let Samuel Kid take the room key. That way, you don't have to open the window and don't have to fake the escape of the killer through the window.\" [1999] I found Poirot's argument convincing. \"Since Richard Nigg locked the door from the inside, how did Samuel Kid get into room 238, take the keys to rooms 121 and 317?\" [2000] \"Yes.\" [2001] \"What if he climbed a tree first and then climbed in through the window?\" [2002] \"Ketchup, think. Jenny Hobbs said she didn't go to the Blaxland Hotel that night, so either Samuel Kid and Richard Nigges collaborated to complete the plan, or the two men didn't cooperate. If they didn't cooperate, Mr. Kid didn't have to climb into Mr. Nigges' room through the window and take those two keys. It doesn't make sense. If these two men really collaborated, Samuel Kid should have put the three keys in Nancy Duane's pocket, not two. And one more thing... if Richard Nigges committed suicide, as you believe, so the cufflinks slid to his throat. But who arranged his body so straight? Do you really believe that a person who took poison could still meticulously design their own death pose? No! It's absolutely impossible.\" [2003] \"I need to think about this again.\" I said. \"You've raised so many new questions that I didn't have before.\" [2004] \"Such as?\" [2005] \"Why did the three victims order sandwiches, cakes, and scones but not eat them? Since they didn't eat, why was there nothing on Ada Gransberry's plate? Where did the food go?\" [2006] \"Ah! Now you're thinking like a real detective. Hercule Poirot is teaching you how to use your gray brain cells.\" [2007] \"Did you think of the food-related questions?\" [2008] \"Of course. I didn't mention the food issue when I asked Jenny to explain the contradictions because I wanted her to think we believed her story. So, I couldn't ask her any questions she couldn't answer.\" [2009] \"Polo! Samuel Kid's face!\" [2010] \"Where is it, my friend?\" [2011] \"No, I don't mean I saw his face. I mean... do you remember when we first met him at the 'Happy Coffee Shop'? He shaved his face, leaving a small patch of stubble with a small wound, while the rest was covered by thick stubble.\" [2012] Poirot nodded. [2013] \"What if the wound we saw wasn't from shaving, but from being scratched by a branch? What if Samuel Kidd scraped his face while climbing into or out of the window of Room 238? He knew he was telling us a lie, that he saw Nancy Duane fleeing the hotel, but he didn't want us to associate the mysterious wound on his face with the tree outside Richard Nigus's room window, so he shaved off a small patch of stubble around the wound.\" [2014] \"So we would think he hurt himself while shaving and only shaved a part of it. Poirot said, \"Then, when he came to see me at his residence, his entire beard was gone, but there were wounds on his face, meant to remind me that he could hurt himself while shaving. Yes, that way, I would think every wound on his face was from shaving.\" [2015] \"Why aren't you excited?\" I asked. [2016] \"Because it's obvious. I came to this conclusion two hours ago.\" [2017] \"Oh.\" I was disappointed. \"Wait, if Samuel Kid's wound was from the tree outside Richard Nigel's room window, then it means he could have climbed into the room, taken the keys to Rooms 121 and 317, right?\" [2018] \"There's no time to discuss what this means now,\" Poirot said sternly. \"We've arrived at the train station. But your question shows that you weren't listening to what I was saying.\" [2019] Dr. Ambrose Flavard was tall and muscular, in his fifties, with black hair that was starting to turn gray at the temples. His shirt was wrinkled and missing a button. Without further ado, he led us into the vicarage. We stood in a cold, large hall with high ceilings and cracked wooden floors. The entire house seemed to have been turned into a makeshift hospital to care for the sole patient. The door opened, and a nurse in uniform emerged. In a different situation, I might have been curious about this arrangement, but now all I could think about was Margaret Ernst. [2020] The entire house seemed to have been turned into a makeshift hospital, for the sole purpose of caring for the only patient. The door opened, and a nurse in uniform emerged. In a different situation, I might have been curious about this arrangement, but now all I could think about was Margaret Ernst. [2021] As soon as we were introduced, I asked, \"How is she?\" [2022] The doctor's face contorted with pain. He composed himself and said, \"She only let me say that she's fine.\" [2023] \"Who?\" Poirot asked. [2024] \"Margaret. She can't tolerate failure.\" [2025] \"You said she wanted to see us, is that true?\" [2026]Dr. Flavard paused for a moment, nodding slightly, saying, \"Most people wouldn't last this long after such a severe attack. Margaret is strong and determined, but the situation is still serious. But damn it, I'll do everything I can to heal her.\" [2027]\"What happened to her?\" [2028]\"Two scoundrels, who ran from the other end of the village to the churchyard at night... oh, I'm ashamed to repeat what they did to the gravestones of the Ives. Margaret heard the commotion outside, she's always alert even when she sleeps, she heard the sound of metal and stones colliding. She rushed out to try to stop them, and they attacked her with the shovel they brought along. They didn't care if they killed her or not! Hours later, the village police arrested them, and everything was cleared up.\" [2029]Polo said, \"Wait a minute, doctor. Do you know who attacked Mrs. Enst? Those two scoundrels... did they confess?\" [2030]\"Of course,\" Dr. Flavard gritted his teeth. [2031] \"So they were arrested?\" [2032] \"Yes, the police have arrested them.\" [2033] \"Who are they?\" I asked. [2034] \"Fred Krieger and Tobias Klatt, father and son. A pair of alcoholics, loafers.\" [2035] I wondered if the son was the loafer I had seen drinking with Walter Stuker at the King's Head Inn. (I later found out that it was indeed him.) [2036] \"They say Margaret obstructed them. As for the graves of the Ives...\u201d Dr. Flavard turned to me, \"Please understand, I'm not trying to blame you, but your visit was the trigger. Someone saw you go to Margaret's cottage, and all the villagers knew she was on the side of the Ives. They knew that Patrick Ives, whom you heard in Margaret's house, was not a lecherous fraud but a victim of cruel persecution and defamation\u2014and the perpetrators were them. This made them want to punish Patrick again. Patrick is dead, and they can't reach him anymore, so they defile his grave. Margaret always said that something like this would happen, so she sat by the window all day, hoping to catch and stop them. Do you know, she never saw Patrick and Francis Ives? Did she tell you? They were my friends. Their tragic fate made me sad, and I always felt guilty about their unjust treatment. And from the beginning, Margaret cared about this matter very much. She was shocked that such a thing happened in the parish her husband managed. She also made her husband care about it. Margaret and Charles coming to Gleeholm is truly fortunate. She is the best ally I could have. No, they are.\" [2037] \"Can I talk to Margaret?\" I asked. If she was dying\u2014although the doctor swore she wouldn't\u2014I had a feeling she had something to say before she passed away. [2038] \"Of course,\" Ambrose Flavard said. \"She would be angry if I didn't let you see her.\" [2039] I, Poirot, and the nurse followed the doctor up a wooden staircase without carpet, entering a bedroom. Margaret Enst's face was wrapped in bandages, covered in blood, bruises, and purple spots. I tried not to show my surprise, but tears welled up in my eyes. [2040] \"Ambrose, have they come?\" she asked. [2041] \"They have.\" [2042] \"Good day, Mrs. Enst. I am Hercule Poirot. I am deeply sorry for what has happened to you.\" [2043] \"Please call me Margaret, Mr. Carruthers. Is Mr. Cattermole here too?\" [2044] \"Yes, I am here.\" I almost couldn't speak. I couldn't imagine anyone being so cruel to a woman. It wasn't something a human would do; it was something a beast, a devil would do. [2045] Margaret asked, \"Are you two being so polite not to scare me? My eyes are swollen shut, and I can't see your faces. Did Ambrose tell you I'm dying?\" [2046] \"No, ma'am. He didn't say that.\" [2047] \"He didn't, did he? Well, but he thinks so.\" [2048] \"Margaret, dear\u2014\"\n[2049] \"He's wrong. I'm too angry to die.\" [2050] \"Do you have something to say to us?\" Poirot asked. [2051] Margaret's throat made a strange sound, almost like a laugh. \"Yes, I do. But I hope you won't be so impatient, jumping right in like that, as if I'm about to drop dead! If that's what Ambrose thinks, then he's mistaken. I need rest now. Today, I'm going to fight death to the bitter end! Ambrose, will you tell them, won't you?\" [2052] \"Yes, if that's what you want me to do.\" His eyes widened with alarm and he took her hand. \"Margaret? Margaret!\" [2053] \"Leave her be.\" The nurse spoke for the first time. \"Let her sleep.\" [2054] \"Sleep.\" Dr. Flavot repeated the nurse's words, his eyes full of confusion. \"Yes, of course, she should sleep.\" [2055] \"Doctor, what does she want to tell us?\" Poirot asked. [2056] \"Can you take the guests to the living room?\" the nurse suggested. [2057] \"No.\" Flavard said, \"I won't leave her. And I need to talk to these two gentlemen in private. Can you give us a few minutes, Nurse Miss?\" [2058] The young nurse nodded and left the room. [2059] Flavard said to me, \"She almost told you everything, about what happened in that dirty village to Patrick and Francis?\" [2060] \"We may know more than you think.\" Poirot said. \"I have spoken to Nancy Duane and Jenny Hobbs, and they told me that the coroner found Patrick and Francis Yves died of accidental poisoning. But Margaret Ernst told Casper that they deliberately took poison to end their lives: first her, then him. The poison used was the toxin of love-apple seeds.\" [2061] Flavard nodded and said, \"That's right. Patrick and Francis both left their last words in the world. I told the police that I believed they died of an accident. I lied.\" [2062] \"Why?\" Poirot asked. [2063] \"Suicide is a sin in the eyes of believers. Patrick's good name was already ruined, and I couldn't stand by and let anyone else harm him. Poor Francis, she didn't do anything wrong, and she was a devout Christian...\"\n[2064] \"Oh, I see.\" [2065] \"I know there are a few people in the village who would be delighted to know that their actions drove the Yves couple to suicide. I won't let them get away with it, especially Harriet Sipper.\" [2066]Poirot said, \"May I ask you something, Dr. Flavard? If I tell you that Harriet Sipper has regretted how she treated Patrick Ives in such a shameful way, would you believe it?\" [2067]\"What do you mean, Poirot? I don't think you're making sense. Harriet would never regret anything she's done, and neither would I. I'm glad I lied sixteen years ago, and I would do it again if I had the chance. Those people who harassed Patrick Ives, led by Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransbury, they're all the devil. I can't think of another word to describe them. As an educated person, I'm sure you're familiar with Shakespeare's The Tempest? There's a line that says, 'Hell is empty and all the devils are here.'\" [2068]\"The next line is 'All the devils are here.'\" [2069]\"That's right. Mr. Carcher, that's why Margaret didn't want you to talk to me. She feels the same way I do, proud of lying for Patrick and Francis, but more cautious. She was afraid I would brag about my actions, like I just did. I know I have to face the consequences. I'll lose my medical license, maybe even my freedom, and I deserve it. I lied, and it killed Charles.\" [2070] \"Charles, Margaret's late husband?\" I asked. [2071] The doctor nodded. \"Margaret and I don't care what people say behind our backs, calling us 'liars,' but Charles did. His health was deteriorating, and if I hadn't been so stubborn in fighting the villains in the village, Charles might still be alive.\" [2072] \"Where are the suicide notes written by the Ivorys?\" Poirot asked. [2073] \"I don't know. I gave them to Margaret sixteen years ago, and I haven't asked her about them since.\" [2074] \"I burned them.\" [2075] \"Margaret.\" Ambrose Vlavard rushed to her bedside. \"You're awake.\" [2076] \"I remember every word on the note. It felt important to remember, so I did.\" [2077] \"You need to rest, Maggie. Talking will tire you out.\" [2078] \"Patrick's suicide note says, 'Tell Nancy I'll always love her.' But I didn't tell Nancy. If I had, it would have exposed Ambrose's lie during the autopsy. However... now the truth is out, Ambrose, you must tell Nancy what Patrick said.\" [2079] \"I will, don't worry, Maggie. I'll take care of everything.\" [2080] \"I'm worried. You haven't told Kitcher and Mr. Poirot about the threats Harry made after Patrick and Francis were buried. Tell them now.\" She closed her eyes again and fell asleep a few seconds later. [2081] \"What threat, doctor?\" Poirot asked. [2082] \"One day, Harriet Sipper came to the vicarage with a dozen or so hooligans, claiming that the villagers of Gleeholm were going to dig up Patrick and Frances' bodies. She said, 'A suicide does not have the right to be buried in such a holy place, it's God's rule.' Margaret walked to the door and said she was talking nonsense, and told them, 'That was the rule once, but it's not anymore. It was abolished in 1880, and it's now 1913. After death, a person's soul is entrusted to the merciful God, and no longer subject to human judgment.'\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nI won't go, you go, and then tell me in detail about your experiences during this trip. In addition, bring two lists: one is the list of guests who checked in at Bluxton Hall Hotel on Tuesday and Wednesday nights, and the other is the list of hotel staff. Check if anyone in the cursed village knows the people on the list, ask about Jane and PIJ. And if you can't find out the reason for the priest couple's death in 1913, don't come back.\" [708] \"Poirot, you have to come with me,\" I said desperately, \"The case at Bluxton Hall Hotel is beyond my abilities, and I rely on you.\" [709] \"My friend, you can continue to rely on me. Let's go back to Mrs. Blanche Answorth's apartment now, and we'll sort out our thoughts so you can be well-prepared.\" [710] He always referred to the place where we lived as \"Mrs. Blanche Answorth's apartment.\" Each time he said this, I would recall that before I considered it a \"home,\" I also thought of it as \"Mrs. Blanche Answorth's apartment.\" [711] \"Sorting out thoughts\" resulted in Poirot standing by the fireplace in the living room, talking non-stop, while I sat beside him and wrote down everything he said. I had never seen anyone speak with such logic, and I never would again. Among the things he had me write down, there were many that I already knew, and I protested, but his lengthy discourse on the importance of methods was very beneficial. It seemed that my needlework-like brain couldn't remember everything, and it was necessary to write it down for future reference. [712] After telling me a long string of known facts, Poirot asked me to write down the unknown facts he hoped to find answers to in the same way. (I was going to put these two long strings of facts here, but I didn't want to bore or anger anyone, so I omitted them.) [713] To be fair, after hastily writing and checking these facts, I did have a clearer understanding: clear, but extremely frustrating. I put down my pen and sighed, \"I don't want to go with all these unanswered questions, and it's likely that I'll never find the answers.\" [714] \"You have no confidence, Katchup.\" [715] \"Yes. What should I do in this situation?\" [716] \"I don't know. I've never encountered this before, and I never worry about encountering problems I can't solve.\" [717] \"Do you think you can find the answers to these questions?\" [718] Polo smiled and said, \"You lack confidence, so you want me to say 'believe me,' is that it? My friend, you know more than you think. Remember the joke you made at the hotel? The one about the three victims all checking in the day before the crime? You said, 'It's like an invitation to come and die together.'\" [719] \"What about it?\" [720] \"Your joke was based on an idea. Planning this murder took more than a day \u2013 traveling by train across the whole country, then dying all in one day, which would be too tense for anyone! But the killer didn't want his target to be too stressed! It's too interesting!\" [721] Poirot stroked his mustache, as if a few laughs could dishevel its neat style. [722] \"My friend, your words also raise doubts: for the victims, preparing to die without effort, and without the killer being so considerate to his target, so why didn't he kill the three guests on Wednesday evening? Instead, he chose to commit the crime on the second night, because it was more convenient for him. He didn't invite the three victims to the Bruxelles Hotel, he just happened to know they were all there and when they were going to be there.\" [723] \"Maybe he was too busy on Wednesday evening?\" [724] \"Why didn't he arrange for the three victims to arrive at the hotel on Thursday morning or afternoon? That way, the killer could still kill them between 7:15 and 8:10 p.m., just as it actually happened.\" [725] I patiently said, \"Poirot, you're making things too complicated. If these three victims knew each other - and we now know they did - they might have had something to do in London that required them to stay for two nights, unrelated to the murder. The killer chose to commit the crime on the second night because it was more convenient for him. He didn't invite the three victims to the Bruxelles Hotel, he just happened to know they were all there, and when they were going to be there. And... I stopped, \"Never mind. It's silly to think that way.\" [726] \"Tell me this silly idea.\" Poirot commanded me. [727] \"Well, maybe the killer is a meticulous planner by nature, and he was worried about the train being late, so he didn't kill the three victims on the day they arrived in London.\" [728] \"It's also possible that the killer was also traveling to London from Gillingham or elsewhere that day, and he or she \u2013 the killer is likely to be a woman \u2013 didn't want to go on a long journey and then have to kill three people.\" [729] \"But the victims could have come back on Thursday, couldn't they?\" [730] \"But they didn't,\" Poirot said calmly. \"We know they arrived the day before, on Wednesday. So, I'm wondering if something happened between the killer and the three victims before the crime. If so, the killer wouldn't have had to come from out of town and could have been living in London.\" [731] \"Possibly,\" I said. \"In fact, these theories are just a roundabout way of saying, 'We don't know what happened and why.' I'm reminded of my initial thoughts on the case. Oh, Poirot\u2026\"\n[732] \"What is it, my friend?\" [733] \"I didn't dare say it before, knowing you wouldn't approve. Those initials on the cufflinks\u2026\"\n[734] \"Ah?\" [735] \"You asked Henry Nigges about PIJ, but I believe the owner of the cufflinks\u2014whomever it is\u2014didn't have the initials PIJ. I think it should be PJI. Look, here,\" I took out a piece of paper and, using my memory to mimic the cufflink's script, drew the three letters, \"can you see it? The letter 'I' is larger, and the 'P's and 'J's on either side are smaller. This is a popular way of engraving initials, with the larger letter representing the surname and placed in the middle.\" [736] Poirot frowned and shook his head, saying, \"Inverting the initials on purpose? I've never heard of such a thing. It's ridiculous!\" [737] \"It's probably a habit. Trust me, my colleagues have used cufflinks like these.\" [738] \"Incredible, the British have no concept of arranging things in order.\" [739] \"Yes, but perhaps\u2026the person we need to ask in Gleghorn is PJI, not PIJ.\" [740] My efforts were in vain, as Poirot immediately understood. \"You, my friend, go to Gleghorn,\" he said, \"Poirot will stay in London.\" [741] Chapter 9\n[742] The Glebeholme Trip\n[743] As instructed, I set off early Monday morning for Glebeholme. My impression of the place was similar to many English villages I had visited before, and there was not much to describe. I suppose there are more differences between cities than between villages, which is why there is more to talk about in cities. For instance, I could have given a detailed account of London's complexity. Perhaps I was just not accustomed to places like Glebeholme, which went beyond my principles\u2014if I had any. [744] I planned to stay at the King's Head Inn, which I was told was easy to find, but I couldn't locate it. Fortunately, a man with freckles on his face, glasses, and a newspaper under his arm appeared out of nowhere and startled me. He asked, \"Lost?\" [745] \"I think so. I'm looking for the King's Head Inn.\" [746] \"Ah!\" He grinned. \"I figured as much, with your luggage and all. You're not from around here, are you? From the street, the King's Head Inn looks like an ordinary house, so you didn't notice. Go down this alleyway, see it? Walk down, turn right, and you'll see the inn's sign and entrance.\" [747] I thanked him and was about to proceed as he suggested when he called me back. \"Where are you from?\" [748] I told him, and he said, \"I've never been to London. What brought you here?\" [749] \"Work,\" I said. \"I don't want to be rude, but I'd like to settle in first. We can chat later.\" [750] \"Oh, I won't keep you then,\" he said. \"What do you do for a living? Oh, I'm starting to ask questions again. We'll talk later.\" He waved and walked off down the street. [751] I made my way back to the King's Head Inn, and he called out behind me, \"Follow the path and turn right.\" He waved cheerfully as he went. [752] He was being friendly and trying to help me, and I should thank him. Normally, I would, but...\n[753] I have to admit, I don't like the countryside. I didn't tell Poirot about it before coming, but on the train, I kept saying it to myself until I got off at that cute little station. I don't like this charming little path we're on, winding around like an \"S,\" with tiny houses on either side that don't seem suitable for human habitation, more like an ancient forest monster's home. [754] I also don't like standing in the middle of the street and being asked impertinent questions by strangers, which makes me sound insincere, because I came to Glessingham to question strangers. [755] The man with the glasses walked away, and all I could hear was my own breathing, with the occasional bird chirping. The houses behind me were surrounded by open fields and distant hills, and the silence made me feel suddenly lonely. Of course, cities can also make you feel alone. In London, you see people going by, but you have no idea what they're thinking. Everyone seems close, yet mysterious. In the countryside, it's the same, but perhaps everyone thinks the same things. [756] The landlord of the King's Head Inn was Victor Muggleton, about fifty, with a sparse growth of white hair on his head and a little redness at the sides of his ears. He seemed to want to talk about London. \"Mr. Cattermole, are you born in London? I beg your pardon for being so impertinent. How many people are there? What is the population? Is it dirty? My aunt went there once and said it was dirty. But I still want to go. I never told her that, and we had a big quarrel. Oh, may she rest in peace! Do all Londoners have cars?\" [757] Fortunately, his volley of questions left me no chance to answer. But when he asked the question he really wanted to know, I couldn't escape. \"Why have you come to Greyladies, Mr. Cattermole? I can't see what there is here that would interest you.\" [758] He stopped, and I had to answer, \"I am a policeman, from Scotland Yard.\" [759] \"A policeman?\" He was still smiling, but his eyes changed: cold, sharp, contemptuous, as if he were probing me, trying to find something to my disadvantage. \"A policeman, why come here? A policeman from London.\" [760] He took my luggage and climbed the winding wooden stairs. He stopped three times, looking back at me, for no apparent reason. [761] I happily discovered that Mr. Mugge's room, unlike the opulent and extravagant room of Blanche Ounsworth's house, was simply decorated and somewhat empty. Thankfully, he didn't prepare a hot water bottle wrapped in a knitted cover for me to put on the bed. I couldn't stand those things; just seeing them irritated me. I believe that on any bed, the warmest thing should be a person. [762] Mr. Mugge also briefly introduced some furnishings that I would not ignore, such as the bed and wooden wardrobe. I tried to look surprised and pleased. Considering that I would eventually have to tell him why I came to Greghollin, I confessed the reason for my visit, hoping to satisfy his curiosity and stop him from looking at me with a scrutinizing gaze. I told him about the murder at the Bluxton Hotel. [763] While listening to my story, his mouth twisted as if he was trying hard not to laugh, but it could also be that I misread the situation. \"You say, murder? In a luxurious hotel in London? More importantly! Lady Siphe and Miss Gransberry were killed? And Mr. Nigges too?\" [764] \"Do you know them?\" I asked as I took off my coat and hung it in the wardrobe. [765] \"Oh, yes, I know them.\" [766] \"I suppose they weren't your friends?\" [767] \"Not friends, not enemies.\" McKin said, \"If you run a hotel, it's the best way to go about it. Friends and enemies only bring trouble. It seems that Lady Brawnshaw and Miss Gransbury, and Mr. Nigus got into trouble.\" [768] I could hear a strange emphasis in his words, what did it mean? Was he expecting something? [769] \"Sorry, Mr. McKin, but... are you happy to hear the news that they three were killed? Or am I overthinking it?\" [770] \"Yes, Mr. Kitcher, you are overthinking it.\" He quickly denied it. [771] We looked at each other for a moment, and I noticed his eyes filled with suspicion, devoid of any emotion. [772] \"I'm just interested in the story you told,\" Mr. Mick said, \"I'm interested in every story told by a guest. It's quite normal for someone running an inn. But who would have thought - a murder!\" [773] I turned my face aside and said firmly, \"Thank you for bringing me to the room, it has been very helpful.\" [774] \"I'm sure you have many questions for me,\" Mr. Mick said, \"I've been running the King's Head Inn since 1911, I'm the best choice.\" [775] \"Oh, of course. But I'd like to unpack, have dinner, and rest first. I don't expect to have a deep conversation with you, but I fear it's inevitable,\" I said, \"Mr. Mick, there's one more thing, very important. Can you please keep what I said just now to yourself? Thank you very much.\" [776] \"Is it a secret?\" [777] \"No, not at all. I just want to say it myself.\" [778] \"You're going to ask them, aren't you?\" said Mickin. \"Nobody in Gleghorn can give you any valuable information.\" [779] \"I don't believe it,\" I said. \"You just said you wanted to talk to me.\" [780] Mickin shook his head and said, \"I don't remember saying that, Mr. Kitcher. I only said you would have questions for me, but I didn't say I would answer them. To be honest... he pointed a skinny, swollen finger at me, \"If you found three dead bodies in a luxurious hotel in London and thought you were a London police officer, then you should go back to London and ask your questions there, not here.\" [781] \"Are you suggesting I leave, Mr. Mikkelsen?\" [782] \"No. It's entirely up to you how you arrange your itinerary. You're welcome to stay if you want to, and it's none of my business.\" [783] I shook my head in confusion. The Viktor Mikkelsen I had just met at the Winger Hotel was a far cry from the one who had greeted me so warmly and had talked so happily about London and his disagreeable aunt. [784] I sat down on the bed and immediately got up again, wanting to go out for some fresh air. If only there were somewhere else to go in Glechov besides the Winger Hotel. [785] I put my coat back on, locked the door, and went downstairs. Mikkelsen was behind the bar, polishing beer glasses, and nodded to me as I came in. [786] A table in the corner was littered with glasses, some empty, some filled with liquor. Two men sat at the table, both seemingly trying to get drunk. They swayed back and forth, yet somehow maintaining perfect balance. One was an elderly man, with a white beard that resembled Santa Claus, while the other was a muscular young man with a square jaw, appearing to be no more than twenty years old. He attempted to speak to the older man, but his words were slurred and incoherent, as if he had consumed too much alcohol. Fortunately, his companion was also inebriated, making it easier to engage in nonsensical conversation rather than engaging in witty banter. [787] Observing the young man, I felt a sense of unease. How had he become so morose? If he didn't change his ways, his face would remain in this state forever. [788] \"Would you like a drink, Mr. Kitcherpool?\" Mickin asked. [789] \"Perhaps later, thank you. I'd like to stretch my legs first.\" I smiled pleasantly, trying to appear amiable even to those I didn't like or trust. While this tactic didn't always work, it occasionally yielded friendly responses. [790] The inebriated young man stood up unsteadily, as if suddenly angered, and I heard him mutter a single word, \"No,\" before stumbling past me and out onto the street. The older man slowly raised his arm \u2013 a movement that took nearly ten seconds \u2013 and pointed at me. \"You.\" [791] I had been in Glehollin for less than an hour, and already two people had rudely pointed at my face. Perhaps this was a gesture of welcome from the locals, although I was skeptical. \"Are you talking to me?\" I asked. [792] Santa Claus spoke, and I sensed that his meaning was: \"Yes, it's you, buddy. Come sit here, sit in that chair, right next to me. Sit where that lazy bum was sitting. Come on.\" [793] Normally, such repetitive speech would have annoyed me, but in this case,